WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.830
- Welcome, everyone,

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.360
and thank you for attending

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.077
the final panel of the conference,

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:14.430
"Political Lotteries:
The Future of Democracy."

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.640
So we decided to end the
conference with this panel

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.030
that allows us and the audience
to have a group brainstorm

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.170
about some of the benefits

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.330
and the challenges of using lotteries,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.610
and also to tackle some bigger questions.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.290
So should we get rid of elections?

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.720
Can deliberative institutions scale up?

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.840
What should new areas of
research and collaboration be?

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.270
This is also going to be the
chance for you, the audience,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.590
to ask questions and
engage with the presenters.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.500
And feel free to ask as many
questions as you'd like,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.320
and there will be individuals
around to pass you a mic.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.750
So once we begin to open Q&amp;A,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.320
please look to Kira in
the back for a microphone.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.930
So we'll begin with a half
hour of prepared questions

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:55.950
and then open it up.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:00.090
But, first, I'm very pleased
to introduce your panelists.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.000
So on the far side, Federica Carugati

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.610
is a Senior Lecturer in
History and Political Economy

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.290
at King's College London.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.300
Her research, as you've seen
earlier in the conference,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.010
focuses on two topics:

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.660
the development of political, legal,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.040
and economic institutions

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.530
and pre-modern
citizens-centered governments,

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.650
and second, the lessons that
the emergence, configuration,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.020
and breakdown of such institutions hold

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.330
for the theory and practice
of institution building

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.470
in the modern world.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.767
She's the author of two books,

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:29.880
"A Moral Political Economy:
Present, Past, and Future,"

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.230
that's Cambridge University Press,

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.850
and "Creating a Constitution:

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.130
Law, Democracy, and
Growth in Ancient Athens,"

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.750
from Princeton University Press,

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.100
as well as a number of articles

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.840
published in academic and popular outlets,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.213
including an article I love in WIRED.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.090
Next to her is Dmitri Landa,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.910
who's a Professor of Politics at NYU.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.280
His research focuses on democratic theory,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.080
formal theory, and political economy,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.000
as well as philosophy and law.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.470
His recent research has focused on

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.510
studying deliberation and debate,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.490
including experimental and formal work

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.320
as to what extent the debate process

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.270
is informative to participants,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.280
as well as research on how to structure

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.380
deliberative activities of argumentation,

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.020
information acquisition, and learning.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.570
Most relatedly, he's
working on a book manuscript

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.797
with Ryan Pevnick, also at NYU, entitled,

00:02:14.797 --> 00:02:17.490
"Representative Democracy:
A Justification,"

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.110
with Oxford University Press.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.360
And this manuscript compares
the electoral selection

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.000
of policymaking officials
to salient alternatives,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.590
many of which we've talked about today,

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.780
such as direct democracy,
lottery-based systems,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.270
and lottocratic alternatives.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.240
Next to Dmitri is Philip Lindsay,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.850
who leads the Democracy Innovation Hub

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.290
at The Hannah Arendt Center

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.170
for Politics and
Humanities at Bard College.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.310
Since 2022, the hub has
hosted two annual convenings

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.570
on citizens' assemblies

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.430
that bring together elected officials,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.340
community leaders,
advocates, and practitioners

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.253
to workshop precisely how to
scale collaborative governance

00:02:53.253 --> 00:02:55.380
from a place-based perspective.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.790
The hub also recently hosted

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.220
the "Doing Democracy
Differently" teacher fellowship,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.720
which trains high school teachers

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.390
on how to bring deliberative
democracy into the classroom.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.310
He's co-founder of the Assembly Project,

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.330
as we've heard about earlier,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.810
and helps coordinate the
Energized New York initiative.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.820
And last but not least, Kris.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.480
Kris Rose is the head of
Governance Insights at Meta,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.070
where he works across the company

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.290
to drive thought on emerging trends

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.380
at the intersection of technology,
society, and governance.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.213
Prior to this role,

00:03:23.213 --> 00:03:25.950
Kris helped launch the
company's Oversight Board,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.020
as well as serving as
a geopolitical analyst

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.840
at the CIA for a decade

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.170
to include secondment as the
president's daily briefer

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.300
to then US President Mike Pence,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.700
and most recently served
as the senior advisor

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.550
at the Cybersecurity and
Infrastructure Security Agency

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.170
during the Biden administration.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.210
Kris holds a master in public
policy from Georgetown,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.320
and is a team member also

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.450
with the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.950
So as you see, we brought together

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.750
a combination of academics,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.640
practitioners, and folks from industry

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.950
to tackle these big questions.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.270
Now, I've given each of the panelists

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.980
the opportunity to have opening statements

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.010
to tell you, the audience,
about their current research

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:03.420
or their current activities.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.760
And we'll start in the
order that I introduced you,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.233
so Federica, please.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.643
- Okay. Is this good? Okay.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.620
So, yeah, Ali asked for us to give

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.500
a little bit of a brief,
I guess, on the research.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.940
Again, I don't wanna bore you too much,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.050
given that some of you at least

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.700
have been in the audience long enough

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.310
to know pretty much what
my research is about,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.550
but just for the sake of
the recording, I suppose,

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.430
I am trained as a historian,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.920
by ambition, a political scientist,

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.020
and I study pre-modern governance,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.300
particularly pre-modern democratic

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.920
or collective governance.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.753
And after spending a,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.270
someone might say, too much time

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.310
thinking about governance
in Ancient Athens,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.400
I decided to broaden the
scope of the investigation

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.220
to studying instances
of collective governance

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.150
in the pre-modern world
that go beyond Athens,

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.270
that go beyond in most recently Europe,

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:04.230
with the hope that broadening the scope

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.450
of the investigation on
historical collective governance

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.090
might give us better, more robust insights

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:12.060
about what collective governance
might look like today.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.650
And so the idea is not
so much to sort of like

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.450
lose myself in historical experiments

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.340
or historical evidence,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.900
but it is to bring that evidence

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.200
to conversations about building
democratic spaces today,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.930
particularly at a time when democracy

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.500
seems to be not as cool an experiment

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.410
as we thought maybe a
couple of decades ago,

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.480
or even less than that.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.330
So, again, I think that just to be

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.190
relatively brief and clear,

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.780
I think that there is a lot to learn

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.430
about historical collective governance

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.600
for building a theory,

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.180
a more robust theory of
collective governance today,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.320
but also to really provide additional

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.810
sort of like a food for thoughts
for all those communities,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.493
many of which we have discussed today.

00:05:56.493 --> 00:06:00.180
They are trying to build
collective governance spaces

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.130
online and offline.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.220
And I think that this is what
my research is trying to do.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.210
Although it is somehow and
often, perhaps too often,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.460
dorking out on things historical,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.510
it is pretty much inspired
by a focus on practice

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.183
and a focus on applications
in the present.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.732
- Great. Thank you, Federica.

00:06:20.732 --> 00:06:22.258
Dmitri?

00:06:22.258 --> 00:06:23.091
- Okay.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.820
I work at the intersection of

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.800
political economy and
political philosophy,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.570
and I want to,

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.403
in my remarks, what I want to do

00:06:31.403 --> 00:06:34.020
is to suggest a
perspective on mini-publics

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.290
and lottocratic governance
that comes from thinking about

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.330
what political economic models

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.510
and the particular strategic
models of decision-making

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.893
tell us about these things.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.950
And I'd like to talk in
particular about two sets

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.450
of political economy models:

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:49.530
models of communication

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.900
and models of delegation.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.050
A key result of the strategic
models of communication,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.150
many of which focus on the scenario of

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.440
committee decision-making,

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.890
committee deliberation prior
to collective policy choice,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.050
is that participant's
willingness to engage

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.450
in a kind of open-ended deliberation

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.390
is critically conditioned
on their expectations

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:10.740
of how their speech might affect

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.340
the ultimate committee decision.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.350
In essence, these models study
the various ways in which

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.420
a participant's communication strategy,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.650
what to say,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.360
how to interpret what others say,

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.490
whether to speak or listen,

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.820
are sensitive to the details
of the policy selection rules

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.140
after communication.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.730
And a key expectation here

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.040
is that participants
behave in a relatively,

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.660
you know, manipulative kind of way,

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.450
not as ideal type participants

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:37.470
in what New York and Harvard most called

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:38.700
ideal speech situation

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:41.733
or Josh called an ideal
deliberative position.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.300
Moving on to the strategic
models of delegation,

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.090
a key result here is that, by and large,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.190
strong accountability incentives

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.390
make for tighter connection

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.563
between the interests of the participants

00:07:55.879 --> 00:07:57.305
and the principles.

00:07:57.305 --> 00:07:58.700
So in the interest of, in particular,

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:01.358
in the interest of the principals voters

00:08:01.358 --> 00:08:04.650
and the choices of those
who are making choices

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.010
on their behalf, which
is to say politicians,

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:12.390
members of legislative
bodies or executives,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.490
there are some exceptions to this,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.960
such as pandering, for example,

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.460
and closely related to that,

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.110
perverse effects of transparency.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.840
But those exceptions have to do

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.580
not with whether you want accountability,

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.890
but whether you want
stricter or weaker incentives

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.780
via the accountability channels.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.850
The bottom line from these kinds of models

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.410
is that accountability

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.690
via selection and treatment mechanisms

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.180
is how you make representation work.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.920
So now let's think about the relation

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.420
between these expectations,

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.610
right from these, you
know, two sets of models:

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.140
strategic models of communication

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.840
and strategic models of delegation

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.390
and the way mini-publics work.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.910
So with respect to the strategic
models of communication,

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.220
you take away the binding policy choice

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.670
and you remove a key
factor that is responsible

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.950
for manipulative communicative posture

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.010
that strategic models of
communication describe.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.890
In other words, by taking away
the binding policy decision,

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.600
you remove a key impediment

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.880
to open-ended deliberative exchange.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.720
Now, there's a worry by
removing an extrinsic motivation

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.280
to effect a certain kind of, you know,

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.770
binding policy outcome as
a result of communication,

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:19.080
you're also removing incentives to

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.840
make careful, kind of
thought out decisions.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.190
But I think what we see
is that citizens juries,

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:27.870
among other types of mini-publics,

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.330
replace extrinsic with
intrinsic motivations.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:31.710
Participants are keen to be there

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:33.090
and to engage with each other.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.490
Of course, this leaves issues on the table

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.990
with respect to the relationship

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.120
between the distribution
of intrinsic motivations

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.400
and the distribution of
participants prior beliefs.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.590
There are demographic
characteristics and so on,

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.500
but these are arguably solvable
kind of technical issues,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.200
and the stratification-based
sample selection techniques

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.590
are, in fact, increasingly
effective in solving them.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.470
If you now go back to the
strategic models of delegation

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.060
that I described,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.550
mini-publics give up
accountability of the represented,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.320
and by doing so, they presumably raise

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:02.610
the specter of decision-making

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.830
that does not represent voters' interests.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:07.320
But because they don't make
binding policy decisions,

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.780
the worry is much more attenuated.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.490
If what we have in mind are

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.250
consultative point of
information kind of bodies,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.720
the concerns with accountability

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.510
are arguably not suppressing.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.130
So both sets of strategic problems

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.470
with communication and with
faithful representation

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.030
appear to be at least attenuated,

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:25.260
if not entirely eliminated

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:28.110
by the lottery-base
mini-publics being deliberative,

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:30.690
but not ultimately policy-making bodies.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.090
So here comes the challenge, right?

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.300
Is this stable in the sense
of equilibrium outcome, right?

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.780
Are we improving and refining
the quality of mini-publics?

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.340
And as we are doing this,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.340
are we not increasing their attractiveness

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:45.900
as substitute policy makers,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.120
or at least as authoritative

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.000
kind of deference-requiring
decision makers?

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.030
This is not the point about
whether scholars or activists

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.100
are seeking to use mini-publics

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.810
as substitutes or supplements

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.420
of existing institutions
of electoral democracy,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.360
rather, this is a point about
the equilibrium authority

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:06.750
with which recommendations of mini-publics

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.310
would be endowed by the citizens,

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:09.930
which is to say, how
the citizens themselves

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.970
would perceive mini-publics.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.620
The more reliable and authoritative

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.090
citizens perceive them to be,

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.830
and so the more differential to them

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.210
the citizens would become,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.340
the stronger the incentives
for the participants

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:22.260
to engage in manipulative speech

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.960
and to depart from faithful
representation become,

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.150
in other words, the more
relevant the strategic models

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.373
of communication and delegation become.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.430
There's a further threat to the stability

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.770
of the normatively attractive
model of mini-publics

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.380
that relates to Ali's and Brenda's work

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.450
that we heard about today.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.160
The more influential the mini-publics are,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:42.690
the more parties want to control them,

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:43.860
because ultimately,

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.020
parties are multidimensional
issue aggregates,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.450
and they're constrained by considerations

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:49.440
of political feasibility

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.350
in the way that deliberative
mini-publics are not parties,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.270
and other long-term decision actors

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.240
should then be naturally
expected to look for ways

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:00.330
to co-opt and constrain
authoritative mini-publics

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:03.660
to avoid being left holding the bag.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.580
So you can think of the
considerations I described

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.710
as a kind of paradox of authority

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.150
for lottocratic mini-publics.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.280
The threat to the
authority of mini-publics

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.440
is from their becoming authoritative.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.960
But if it is a paradox, it's not the kind

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.520
that comes with an absurd proposition

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.550
since we can readily make
sense of it analytically.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.260
Whatever it is, I think it raises

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.410
a set of important questions
that is worth thinking about,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.890
whether expectations from the many

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:30.510
existing mini-public experiments

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:34.170
can be expected to scale up
and persist over the long run.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.520
What we can plausibly hope,

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.230
there are also mini-publics to be,

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.620
and, you know, in the long run,

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.853
how enthusiastic about them,
ultimately, we should be.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:44.550
- Thank you, Dmitri.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:45.383
Phil, go ahead.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:48.480
- Fascinating. I feel like I need

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:49.920
five minutes to just reflect on it.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.620
I don't know if this is on, but...

00:12:53.367 --> 00:12:54.917
Am I loud enough or should I...

00:12:56.613 --> 00:12:58.173
- Now, it's on. There we go.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.080
Yeah, that was fascinating
to think through.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:03.900
And...

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.460
So my name's Philip Lindsay.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.230
I lead the Democracy Innovation Hub

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:10.323
at The Hannah Arendt Center, Bard College.

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.370
I found myself on the way up
to The Hannah Arendt Center

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:16.590
to a conference that they were running

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.843
before I joined two and a half years ago.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.650
The Hannah Arendt Center
has been running conferences

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.110
for the past 15 years on different topics:

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:28.620
social media, civil disobedience.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.410
We bring together journalists, activists,

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.860
elected officials, writers, thinkers

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:36.240
for a couple days every year.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.510
And for two years, from 2020 and 2021,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.640
the theme of the conference was sortition

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.023
and spaces of power.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.410
And...

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.390
I was on my way up.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.340
I signed up to attend the conference.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.200
As someone who was on their way

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.080
to become a social studies teacher,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.130
I was thinking of...

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.710
I just quit my job at a
community outreach center

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.750
in Sunset Park, Brooklyn, where I live,

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.020
worked at a community health
center for three years,

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:05.940
and was ready to go back into teaching.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.530
I thought that was the best way for me

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:11.610
to be working with young people on civics

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:13.860
and getting young people
engaged in democracy.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:15.310
That's really what drives me.

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.440
This conference blew my mind

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.420
because I had no idea about the history

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.613
of random selection, sortition,

00:14:25.109 --> 00:14:26.970
and it, you know, it made
me feel quite ignorant,

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:27.960
but it's also a failure

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.570
of our public school system, I think.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.670
And it totally energized me.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:36.480
I was feeling quite down
about the possibilities

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:40.770
for comprehensive approaches and solutions

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.900
to what we are facing as a society.

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.450
I felt like our society
was collapsing around me.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:46.650
I feel like we were not coming up

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.710
with the sort of collaborative
problem-solving mechanisms

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.330
that we desperately needed.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:53.970
And when I started learning
about these assemblies

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:55.713
at the conference,

00:15:00.052 --> 00:15:01.410
I was re-energized.

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.320
And for the past two and
a half years, essentially,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.930
basically, all of my
time has been dedicated

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:10.050
to organizing a space at the Arendt Center

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:11.910
called the Democracy Innovation Hub

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:14.280
that tries to energize others

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.370
around these same issues of same model.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:20.400
Although I would say we're
not obsessed with one model.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:23.490
We don't think citizens'
assemblies are gonna save the world

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:25.530
or that there's one type of as assembly

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:27.430
that should be implemented everywhere.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.770
I think what inspired me
and re-energized me was

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.290
the idea of bringing everyday people

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:38.670
not just to get to come
into a room and talk,

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:39.810
but to have a sho...

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.780
The question that really
has inspired me is,

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:45.330
if you were to design
an ideal learning space

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.270
that empowered people to take
action over their own lives

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:51.090
and the lives of their communities,
what would it look like?

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.610
I think if you try to answer that,

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:58.610
you get something towards a
citizens' assembly of some sort.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.700
This idea of bringing people together

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.250
and intentionally mixing in the society,

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.020
intentionally mixing the society,

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:09.420
getting people to see
other parts of the society

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.003
that they are not
interacting with every day,

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.250
from all kinds, you know,

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.010
crossing barriers and neighborhoods,

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:18.570
is something that inspired me.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:19.403
And...

00:16:19.403 --> 00:16:22.470
So what have we been doing at the center?

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.080
We've run three major convenings.

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:28.110
The first one was 2022.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:33.110
We had over 50 participants up
in the Hudson Valley at Bard.

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.240
And the intent, similar to an assembly,

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.860
is that we try to really
bring together people

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.900
that are maybe not talking to each other,

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:42.540
but are somewhat, could be primed

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:44.670
or could be interested in this model.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.240
And we say, "Let's work
together to scale some of these

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.820
and co-design them from a
place-based perspective."

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.350
That is, let's not just copy

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:55.710
what X, Y, and Z country
or place is doing.

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:56.970
Let's ask what would it mean

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:59.340
to rebuild trust in our locality

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:00.600
from the ground up,

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.210
but also from the inside
out and the outside in.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:04.080
So we've done that,

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:05.460
we've been doing that
in the Hudson Valley.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:06.990
And so there's working groups

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:09.780
of both elected officials
and everyday people

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:12.430
who have come together to
start exploring these ideas

00:17:13.305 --> 00:17:14.220
in and around Bard.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.590
And then we've also done that
in New York City where I live.

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:18.213
You heard from Forest earlier.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:23.490
Essentially, we've run
these two convenings

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.740
and one teacher training workshop,

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:29.580
and the last convening
really brought together

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:31.440
the teachers with the elected officials

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:32.430
and community leaders.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.880
So we had over 140 people in New York City

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.653
just a few weeks ago,

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:42.210
and it was a really inspiring
dynamic group of people

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.280
exploring models from all over the world.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.010
But then importantly, it wasn't just,

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.450
it's like, how do we model this?

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:49.620
Like if you even think about

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.480
the way this room is structured,

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:53.190
the way we we're doing public meetings,

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:54.870
the way we do conferences,

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:58.050
if we start thinking with
a design perspective,

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:00.240
we could redesign the way we're doing

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.930
a lot of these convenings
to workshop these ideas.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.030
Now, again, this is not coming
from a research perspective.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:06.990
This is like...

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.120
I'm way on the applied side, right?

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.340
But the idea is to bring,

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:15.153
to have it be a mix of
academics, practitioners,

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.480
advocates, students, young people.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.700
They should be in the room
co-designing these processes,

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:22.170
thinking through how they might work,

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:23.620
how they're affected by them.

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:27.930
And so that last convening we've just done

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.570
really is a culmination of a
lot of the work we're doing.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:31.590
Training teachers,

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:33.510
doing workshops with city agencies,

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.040
trying to figure out

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:37.890
how a process might
fit into a city agency,

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.413
how a city agency could pilot a process.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:45.360
And so we workshop two live
initiatives in New York City

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:46.193
in this last convening.

00:18:46.193 --> 00:18:48.330
One was Energizing New York

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:49.800
that you heard about earlier today,

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.640
and another one was a possible
assembly on some topic

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.440
with the Mayor's Office of Engagement

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:56.290
in New York City.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:00.340
So that's kind of our model is like

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:02.850
you build a space, and
then you ask people,

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:06.713
you invite people in to
co-design the assembly

00:19:06.713 --> 00:19:08.700
or the series of assemblies with you.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:10.170
The last thing I would just say is that

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:11.370
we're at The Hannah Arendt Center,

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:15.330
and there is a deep theoretical
connection to her work

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.390
and her, you know, that
conference that inspired me.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:22.500
This idea of it being a deeply human thing

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:24.210
to need to be seen in public,

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.670
to need to appear in
public with other people,

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:30.734
and to take action with other
people as being deeply human

00:19:30.734 --> 00:19:33.060
is one of the reasons
we're at the Arendt Center.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:34.770
And, yeah, I'm happy to be here.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:36.780
I hope I didn't go over my time.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:37.950
- You're good, you're great.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:40.177
Finally, Kris.
- I'll take this out.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:42.153
Great.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:45.090
Thank you. It is wonderful to be here,

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:48.450
and I'm really grateful
to be learning from folks

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.950
that are so studied

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:52.770
and such deep experts on this issue

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.250
on these various approaches to democracy.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.230
And thank you, Ali, for inviting me

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.390
and also for hosting this.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:03.060
I have found it to be so interesting,

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:04.590
and as the neophyte in the room,

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:06.090
I think the resident neophyte,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.540
I'm thrilled to kind of hear about

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.041
all the various innovative approaches

00:20:12.041 --> 00:20:14.280
that governments, that organizations,

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:15.690
and that certainly researchers

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.780
are taking to innovate democracy.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.110
So I come, as Ali mentioned, from Meta,

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:25.590
and I'm thrilled to be
here because at Meta,

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:29.700
we are taking the first
steps that we believe

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:32.670
are important to democratize
inputs into our innovation.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:34.860
And so my team is the governance team.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.740
We sit at the intersection
of technology and society.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.110
Our mission is to really
elevate user voice

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:41.490
into our innovation.

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:43.170
And so our flagship initiative,

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.120
which we launched three years ago is,

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:46.380
as Ali mentioned,

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:47.700
is called the Oversight Board.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.460
It's been termed the
Supreme Court for Facebook,

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:51.870
but essentially, it is a recourse

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.910
or redressal mechanism

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.400
that everyday users can appeal to.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:00.600
It's an independent institution
that we established,

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:04.620
but we have enshrined
through a series of bylaws

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:05.670
and governing documents

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.680
that has independent decision-making

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.930
and binding authority over Meta

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:12.420
when it comes to individual's content.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:14.610
So if you use one of Meta's platforms

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:15.960
and you disagree with the decision

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.430
that we've taken on your content,

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:18.960
for the first time in human history,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.090
you can appeal to an external body

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.220
that has binding authority
over the company.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:26.170
And so we think that that's
a really important first step

00:21:27.614 --> 00:21:30.210
in governing the company
and divesting power away

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:33.060
from a small set of
individuals at one company

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:37.350
to have such an impact on things
like freedom of expression.

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:39.060
But we also recognize that, fundamentally,

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:41.730
that is a redressal mechanism
or recourse mechanism,

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:46.560
and so we have been experimenting,
over the past two years,

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.710
in deliberative discourse
and deliberative democracy

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:54.360
to democratize inputs into
our innovation at the onset.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:56.100
So we've been really, really thrilled

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.110
to start taking steps in this direction

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:02.220
and to iterate throughout each pilot

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:05.280
that we've pursued.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.400
About two years ago,
we ran our first pilot

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.590
that was focused on what we should do

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.710
on climate misinformation on our platform.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.320
The second pilot that we
held was about a year ago,

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.830
and it was on specific policies
related to the Metaverse.

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:22.600
And then most recently,
we were thrilled to host

00:22:23.437 --> 00:22:25.740
a community forum, that's our term for it,

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:28.470
on generative AI and the
principles that should underpin

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.780
how we innovate our
generative AI products.

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:33.480
And so I can share just a
little bit about the approach.

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.790
Each one has been slightly different.

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:37.320
But we partner,

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:39.840
as a company, we partner with

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.360
Stanford's Deliberative Democracy Lab

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:44.010
to do a deliberative poll.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.740
So that's actually the
format of the mechanism.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.290
And then we also partner with
the Behavioural Insights Team,

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.630
a behavioral economics organization

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:55.650
and social science firm, to help us design

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:57.540
and to also vet our materials,

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:59.190
and our approach, and our design

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.760
to ensure it's as free
as possible from bias.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.880
And so the way that the process works,

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:08.280
and we've been really pleased to date,

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:11.040
but we're also really open
to innovating further,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:14.280
is that we seek out a
representative sample of the public.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:15.990
We use a polling firm,

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:18.930
so people don't need to actually
be users on our platforms.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:22.290
They don't need to have
any specific expertise.

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:24.450
We go out and recruit everyday people

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:26.520
that want to participate.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:30.690
We then provide them an education packet

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.690
with background on the topic

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.750
that we are seeking their input on.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:40.380
That packet has background context.

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:43.170
It's written at an accessible level.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.120
We work with Behavioural
Insights Team to do that,

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.100
to make it tangible and accessible.

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:49.470
And then we provide that packet

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:51.660
as a pre-read to participants.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:56.430
Participants then join the
forum remotely and virtually

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.040
through Stanford's deliberative platform

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:00.870
that is AI facilitated,

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:05.040
and guides them through,
without human moderation,

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.080
guides the participants through

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:09.360
a series of questions and proposals.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.570
So we may have an overarching topic

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.760
that we want their feedback on,

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.340
but we provide, in the education material,

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.440
a series of different ideas

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:21.810
for how we might approach
a particular problem

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:24.780
and the associated pros and cons

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:27.810
to unpack the tensions that are inherent

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.460
within that particular topic.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:33.960
So, for example, on the most
recent forum on generative AI,

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.120
we wanted to hear people's perspectives

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:40.380
on how chatbots should foster
connection with humans.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:45.180
It's a major topic because
chatbots have such great promise,

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.220
and generative AI has such great promise

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.850
to alleviate symptoms of loneliness.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:53.730
There's a growing, small
but growing body of evidence

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:57.540
about its utility and
cognitive behavioral therapy.

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:01.800
But at the same time,
there's also real risks

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:03.720
for people engaging with chatbots.

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:05.490
There was one famous case in Europe

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.590
in which one individual committed suicide

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.120
after engaging a chatbot,

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.070
and it encouraged him to commit suicide.

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.760
And so these are technologies
that are transformative

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:18.000
but also have real risk for offline harm.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.160
And so we wanted to ask participants about

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:21.930
what they thought on how chatbot

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.030
should establish connections with humans.

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:25.020
And as a part of that,

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:26.670
we would unpack the pros and the cons

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:29.130
of the series of proposals.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:31.200
The participants enter this platform,

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:32.280
they engage with one another,

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:34.350
they debate and share their perspectives.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:38.550
We then provide access
to a panel of experts

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:40.320
that have technical expertise,

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.510
human rights expertise,
research expertise,

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.010
and the participants are
able to ask questions

00:25:45.941 --> 00:25:47.010
of these experts.

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.320
And then there's a session in which

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.750
participants then leave the expert panel,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.040
can debrief with one another

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:54.510
and share what they learned.

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:57.180
And the way that we actually
solicit their direct input

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:58.050
is through a deliberative,

00:25:58.050 --> 00:25:59.610
it is a deliberative poll.

00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:02.850
And so they take a poll
before and after the forum

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:05.730
so that we can understand how
their views may have changed.

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:08.070
And so we think that this has been

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:09.780
kind of a really great experiment

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.610
in deliberative discourse.

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:14.730
Our first kind of
learnings from the pilots,

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:18.180
we're still penning the results
from this particular forum,

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.590
but that we've been really thrilled

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.990
that people, first of all,

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.450
as I think evidenced by all the research

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:26.070
and everything that's been shared here,

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:27.930
people are willing and they're able

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:29.850
to engage deeply with one another

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:31.320
on very complex issues.

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:35.820
And so it gives us great
hope that people can discuss

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.110
some of the most pressing
issues of our time

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:41.670
and develop really
rich, meaningful results

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:42.783
through deliberation.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:45.570
The the other learning is that

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:47.130
people appreciate having a voice.

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.470
And that's something that we have found

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:50.430
with the Oversight Board

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:52.470
in terms of empowering people to appeal

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:55.170
to a body that can overrule Meta.

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:56.247
But we also recon,

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:59.610
and I think the data to date in our forms

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.380
suggests that people actually,

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:06.380
there's a positive change
in which they have cited

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:10.320
that they have been really
appreciating the experience.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.270
But we also know that these,

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:14.760
the legitimacy of such
systems only matter,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.830
and we've heard this
throughout the day-to-day,

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:19.110
only matter when there's
actually tangible outcomes

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:20.730
that come from these forums.

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:22.800
And so whereas our Oversight Board

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:24.870
has binding authority over Meta,

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:27.120
this model is advisory in nature.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.910
And so what we are doing, moving forward,

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:31.800
is developing a much more robust way

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:34.500
that we can share how
the results of the forum

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:37.500
and how the feedback that we
receive from the participants

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:40.260
actually will inform our
innovation moving forward.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.713
And then lastly, I would
just say that we believe,

00:27:44.699 --> 00:27:45.630
through this particular model,

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.880
that technology offers a great opportunity

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:50.220
to scale discourse.

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:53.940
And I know a lot of the
deliberative assemblies

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:56.400
in history have been in person.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.380
Given Meta's scale of having

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.380
over 3 billion users on our platforms,

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:03.450
and given the global
nature of the company,

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:05.310
we really are looking to technology

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.950
to be something that can be an enabler

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:10.770
at the same time that
creates its own set of risks.

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:12.360
Ali was one of our observers

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:14.040
at the forum a couple weeks ago,

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:15.630
and we've had great conversations,

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:17.790
and she's offered great feedback about

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.910
the unevenness that comes
inherent with technology.

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:22.470
So there's this great promise of scale,

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:26.400
but also potential risk
of uneven discourse.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.590
And so we're just really looking forward

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:29.970
to continuing to innovate further.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:31.890
I think I really appreciate the spirit of

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:32.910
kind of, throughout the day,

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:34.950
people talking about different models,

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:36.030
and what might work here

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:37.890
might not work in another context,

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:40.050
and what may have worked in the past

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:41.760
may not necessarily work in the future.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.430
And so we're very open-minded
to continuing to innovate,

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.830
but I think our mission,
really, moving forward,

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:49.830
is to continue to solicit input

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:52.860
and to democratize
inputs into the company.

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:53.910
Thanks.

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:55.530
- Excellent. Thank you, Kris.

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:57.510
As you can see, we constructed the panel

00:28:57.510 --> 00:28:59.610
with a wide range of viewpoints.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:01.800
And so now we're going to discuss

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.570
a lot of pressing issues about lotteries

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:07.080
that tap into each of the
panelists' individual expertise.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:08.310
We're gonna start with the big one,

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:10.200
kind of the elephant in the room,

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:13.500
which is, assume that democracy is dying

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:15.360
and elections are broken.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:16.950
Should we just get rid of elections?

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:18.540
Should we make a radical change

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.270
and stop electing our politicians,

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:22.260
so our congressmen

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.780
or our parliamentarians,
if you're in Europe,

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:28.080
and switch to a
sortition-based institution?

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.480
Is that possible? Should we do it?

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:31.520
Dmitri or Federica,

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:33.540
if one of you wanna take this one.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:34.653
- Okay, I'll start.

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:36.900
I have a sense that, you know,

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:40.563
defenses of mini-publics
often have two components:

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.995
a kind of a very pessimistic,

00:29:43.995 --> 00:29:45.420
almost mordant element,

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:46.740
and highly optimistic,

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:48.780
you know, sometimes even joyous one.

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:50.790
The pessimistic has to do

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:52.790
with the existing electoral institutions

00:29:53.917 --> 00:29:55.620
and the optimistic one has to do with,

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.350
you know, wonderful replacements
that are being suggested,

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.150
including, of course,
lottocratic institutions.

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:02.220
And I'm not sure that this is

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.353
an apples to apples comparison.

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:07.950
I think there's a kind of a
tendency that goes with this,

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.110
of course, to see the status quo,

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:11.760
to treat the status quo institutions

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:16.080
as the proper benchmark version
of representative democracy.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:17.320
This is it, folks, right?

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:18.453
That, like that.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:21.480
And if it is, then maybe

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:22.980
we should get rid of elections, right?

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:24.870
Maybe that's right.

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:26.570
But that seems a little too hasty.

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:30.720
And I don't think that
the status quo version

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.190
of representative democracy

00:30:32.190 --> 00:30:34.533
is the proper benchmark version,

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:38.010
and so I do think it's a comparison

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:39.360
of an apple to an orange,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:43.170
a kind of inferior institutional
form of electoral regime

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:46.770
to potentially superior,
or arguably superior,

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.290
within the set of lottocratic regimes

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:50.707
on institutional form of another,

00:30:50.707 --> 00:30:52.950
you know, if we're
talking about lottocracies

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:55.230
or if we're talking about
modified direct democracies,

00:30:55.230 --> 00:30:57.420
some kind of modified
direct democracy version,

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:00.300
which is to say, I don't think
that's a fair comparison.

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:01.890
There are many ways, it seems to me,

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:02.790
in which we might go about

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:05.340
reforming electoral systems from within

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:09.690
without giving up on the idea
of electoral representation

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.250
or of political accountability

00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:14.130
on which the idea of electoral
representation rests.

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:16.920
One important principle
of institutional design

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:19.980
that I think kind of
speaks to many concerns

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:23.550
that lottocratic critics
of elections have raised

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:25.350
is that it's a very intuitive idea

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.150
that we should seek to minimize

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:29.790
the influence of the political actors

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:32.193
over the rules of their
own political contests.

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:34.350
The basic intuition there

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:36.870
is that it's precisely
that kind of influence

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:40.530
that gives rise to
corrupted electoral system,

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:42.360
and of course, very few
electoral regimes today

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.610
come anywhere close to
implementing this principle.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:46.260
So it seems to me that there are,

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:47.400
and this is just one example,

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:48.720
but it seems to me that
there are many ways

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.060
in which we might look
to institutionalized

00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:53.130
something like this,
including, by the way,

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:55.140
via something like,

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.510
you know, randomly selected
electoral commissions

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:00.480
that Christian was speaking
about earlier today.

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:01.980
So there's a lot of room for innovation

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:04.100
and improvement from within
the electoral system.

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:05.853
So I think it's too hasty.

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:09.870
- Yeah, I agree with that.

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:12.394
And I'm just gonna add one thing.

00:32:12.394 --> 00:32:13.260
I don't think,

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:17.760
I guess I'm challenging the
premise on a different aspect,

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:19.080
I don't think that democracy is dy,

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:20.070
if democracy is dying,

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:21.540
I don't think that democracy is dying

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:22.920
because of elections,

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:25.140
or uniquely because of elections.

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:28.980
And what I see in the
research that I conduct on

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:31.860
pre-modern democracies
or collective governance

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:33.000
is that elections are, in fact,

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:36.450
an element in the soup
of collective governance.

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:39.660
And so what seems to me, urgent, however,

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:42.090
is to question the role of elections

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:44.140
as a unique sort of like a form

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:48.630
for eliciting the popular preferences

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:51.060
and to identify the proper space

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:54.630
for electoral politics
vis-a-vis lottocratic policy

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:57.240
or other types of policy.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.530
The problem, however, seems to me,

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:01.380
and I think that the
discussion that we had today

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:04.680
sort of like cemented some of the ideas

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:07.830
that I arguably came into
this conference with,

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:10.440
which is that we really
lack the social science

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:11.430
to address this question

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:13.830
to properly understand the relative value

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:17.880
and the scope of different
types of selection mechanisms.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:22.880
And what worries me is that,
again, as a social scientist,

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:27.210
I think that we do really
need to put some of our brains

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:29.670
onto this question of trying to understand

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:33.660
the relative scope and value
of different mechanisms.

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:36.600
But listening to, you know, practitioners,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.520
I wonder if, you know,

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:41.250
how the social science
actually needs to be integrated

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:44.130
and integrate with experiments,

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:46.350
particularly, experiments
where we see like

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.667
sort of an attempt to just
come into the room, saying,

00:33:48.667 --> 00:33:51.330
"Forget what we know about the past,

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:53.430
let alone what happened five minutes ago."

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:56.760
Then to think about like how
to co-design these spaces,

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:59.550
I'm really of two minds
about what the relative value

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:01.590
of each approach might be,

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:04.040
and I think that, ultimately,
we might need both.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.230
- And, you know, both
panelists bring up a good point

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:09.060
in that there are many
ways to use lotteries.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:11.400
And actually, our ability
as social scientists

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:13.800
to collect empirical data
on the highest levels

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.120
is really hard, right,

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.070
because these are governing institutions

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:18.300
with real life politicians,

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:19.920
parties that are established.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:23.160
But we see a new wave,
a deliberative wave,

00:34:23.160 --> 00:34:26.220
of institutions being
implemented all over.

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:27.540
So citizens' assemblies,

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:30.240
or redistricting, or Oversight Boards.

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:32.100
And so maybe this is the future.

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:36.180
And I was wondering, Phil,
could you maybe argue for

00:34:36.180 --> 00:34:38.370
including more deliberative institutions

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.760
or the types of activities
that your organization does?

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:42.593
Where should we start?

00:34:42.593 --> 00:34:44.490
If not at the very top,
where should we start?

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:45.690
At maybe local levels,

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:47.610
and getting local governments involved

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:48.910
to incorporate lotteries

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:51.483
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:56.250
The way I've been thinking about this

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:58.440
and the way I think a lot of people...

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:01.020
I mean, I say a lot like
the handful of folks

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:03.783
who are also in educational institutions,

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:06.780
which are naturally anchor institutions

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:08.190
in communities, right?

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:12.630
They order food from a
local catering company,

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.250
they serve students, right,

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:16.200
especially if they're a public school.

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:20.520
Is how do you think about re, you know,

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:22.320
reinvigorating local democracy

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:24.123
from a place-based perspective?

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.460
What I've been really
inspired by is this idea

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:31.800
that Federica kind of hinted at,

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.140
which is experimenting

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:37.290
and collaborating with both academics,

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:38.640
but then, you know,

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.530
local teachers, community leaders,

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:42.090
on some hard issue

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:43.650
that's actually happening
in the community.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:44.820
So for instance,

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:47.280
in Lancaster, Pennsylvania

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:49.500
at Franklin &amp; Marshall,

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:52.740
they've been running
deliberative forums there,

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:55.560
you know, at the university
space for the past year.

00:35:55.560 --> 00:35:58.410
I only found out about
this a couple weeks ago,

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:00.187
but I was immediately inspired to say,

00:36:00.187 --> 00:36:02.730
"Yeah, that's a great model
that we can take back to Bard.

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.610
We have not been running
deliberative forums."

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:08.550
A big part of what the way I see these

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:09.900
and the way we see them
at the Arendt Center

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:11.550
is as schools,

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:13.470
as like gyms for democracy, right?

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:15.240
Again, I said, if you're
gonna design a space

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:17.130
that gives people the practical tools

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:18.120
for practicing democracy,

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.400
it would look something
like a citizens' assembly.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:21.233
So...

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:24.900
I mean, sitting in a classroom

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:26.550
and learning about civics

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:28.080
in a passive way is just it.

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:30.303
I don't know if it was
ever a good idea, you know?

00:36:30.303 --> 00:36:32.130
I don't know if that was ever design...

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:33.540
I don't know if it was
good for the 20th century,

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:34.620
I doubt it.

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.800
I think the things that
inspired me to be a citizen

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:40.320
were going out and working on campaigns

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.360
that mattered to me when I was 17.

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:44.400
That's why I'm still involved in poli,

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:46.530
you know, policy or democracy in some way.

00:36:46.530 --> 00:36:49.110
And so if you give young
people, especially,

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:50.430
but also just community members,

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:52.500
a different way to engage,

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:53.340
and you empower them,

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.350
and you also mix them with other folks

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.540
that they wouldn't normally encounter,

00:36:57.540 --> 00:36:59.670
through some deliberative experience,

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:00.840
but in a very structured way,

00:37:00.840 --> 00:37:03.900
again, I think, like, we're often blind

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:07.530
to the amazing sort of capacity of,

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:10.170
our human capacity to facilitate.

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:11.520
We don't engage in that.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.350
We don't build that muscle in our society.

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:15.510
Like, we don't build that
collaborative muscle.

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:17.190
And I think if we started to think about

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:20.250
what would it mean to give young people

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:22.140
the muscles and the capacity

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.270
to facilitate dialogue in their community,

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:25.710
like that's a win no matter what.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:27.120
We can start doing that.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:28.410
I don't, I mean, maybe there's a...

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:29.820
I'd love to hear
counterargument against this,

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:32.520
but, like, problem solving,

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:33.873
active listening,

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.560
psychosocial skills that enable people

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:39.090
to collaborate on better problems.

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:42.000
Like, I'm sure Meta would
love to hire more people

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:42.990
that are good at that, right?

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:45.120
So I think if we start doing that,

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:46.530
as educational institutions,

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:50.220
on real existing projects, locally,

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:51.840
partnering with elected officials

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.960
who want to facilitate some
deliberative processes,

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:57.480
not just deliberations,
but also leading to action,

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:59.040
that's the way we've
been thinking about it

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:00.180
from the Democracy Innovation Hub

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:02.610
is like bringing all those
folks that we can together,

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:04.080
being anchored in one place.

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:06.210
So not trying to say like,
"This is some solution,"

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:08.250
but bringing people in
to think through this.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.200
And be inspired by the
international examples.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.940
Like, that's the one
thing we've really seen

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:13.200
that when you bring people in

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:14.857
and give them case studies of like,

00:38:14.857 --> 00:38:16.950
"Look, Bogota in Columbia is doing this.

00:38:16.950 --> 00:38:18.270
Like, why don't we try it?" Right?

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:19.680
Like, people need that right now.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.270
People are, especially young people,

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:23.370
are deeply cynical about
the systems we're in.

00:38:23.370 --> 00:38:26.088
So we need to come up with ways

00:38:26.088 --> 00:38:29.730
to give them pathways to action

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:30.843
that are not just,

00:38:32.167 --> 00:38:34.267
"look at how bad everything is," you know?

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:37.410
- Okay. So we need to be more optimistic

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.270
and give the youth of today

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:41.400
more opportunities to
get involved in politics

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:42.450
that may be non-traditional.

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:43.860
So it's not just go vote,

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:46.740
but it's, you know,
develop policy positions

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:47.970
with people you might never have

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:49.410
encountered before.
- Can I say

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:50.310
one more thing about that?
- Yeah, please. Yeah.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:51.360
- Okay. So that's one thing, right?

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:52.620
I've worked on a lot of campaigns,

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:54.780
and I've been burnt out by campaigns.

00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:56.430
When you work in this political system,

00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:58.080
you try to achieve a certain goal,

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:00.247
and your whole outlook is,

00:39:00.247 --> 00:39:02.040
"I'm gonna try to get as many votes

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:03.690
or as many X, Y, Z,

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:06.840
and then it's all or nothing."

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:07.770
That, it's good...

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:09.210
Sometimes, it is all or nothing,

00:39:09.210 --> 00:39:10.470
and it's good to have that spirit,

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:11.640
but, like, especially young people

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.410
are craving that sort of,

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:14.850
Christian, in your presentation,
you mentioned that,

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:16.800
like the call to action,
the public service.

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:18.667
We are just failing to say,

00:39:18.667 --> 00:39:20.910
"To have ambition about
what it would look like

00:39:20.910 --> 00:39:22.980
if we called the public to action

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:25.500
in a non-dogmatic ideological way,

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:26.550
in a problem solving way."

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.620
I think, so I think...

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:29.610
Yeah, I think that aspect

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:31.050
of like getting young people involved

00:39:31.050 --> 00:39:34.770
in re-energizing democracy
that isn't just about

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:37.050
a specific campaign or
a specific individual,

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:38.490
like getting someone elected,

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:39.323
that's necessary.

00:39:39.323 --> 00:39:40.680
I'm absolutely against replace,

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:42.450
like, getting rid of elections,

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:44.580
like, or having that framework right now.

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:47.160
But I think that model
does lead to burnout

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:48.060
and disappointment

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:51.030
because if you think
you're gonna win everything

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:52.350
by getting one person in office,

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:53.700
like, you're delusional.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:55.140
But young people want to be a part

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:57.060
of those kind of campaigns.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:58.410
- No, I think that's absolutely right.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:00.690
I'm gonna use that as
a segue to talk about

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:04.050
how not just young people,
but society is changing

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:05.130
and that we're going digital.

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:07.290
We got our information
from different sources.

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:09.420
We just, you know,
lived through a pandemic

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:13.650
where Zoom and other kind of
forms of digital communication

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:15.480
became our lifeline for a while.

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:17.700
And what's interesting
if we think about scale

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:21.990
and how to involve more people
in deliberative activities,

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:24.450
I think, actually, Meta is a incredibly,

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:26.910
a fascinating example of this.

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:28.620
Now, I got to be an observer,

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:32.190
and it was a remarkably well run platform.

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:34.920
The groups were engaging.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:36.570
You know, the groups kinda acted,

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:37.650
if you teach, right,

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:40.200
how do you get students to
discuss in a deliberate forum?

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:42.120
You know, how do you get
someone from Florida,

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:43.740
someone who's Zooming in from New York,

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:47.250
someone who's from a, you
know, who worked in AI

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.380
versus who's worried about
losing their job in AI.

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:51.630
It was a fascinating experience,

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:53.760
and maybe the future, right?

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:56.340
So if Meta is experimenting,

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:57.540
doing this at scale,

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:00.480
and bringing deliberative institutions

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:03.120
into a different, to industry,

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:05.310
not democracy, right, to industry,

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:07.500
I think that's incredibly promising.

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:10.946
So I wanna hear a little
bit more, Kris, about,

00:41:10.946 --> 00:41:12.300
you know, do you think
that this is something

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:13.890
Meta will continue with?

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:15.480
Obviously, you've had positive experience,

00:41:15.480 --> 00:41:18.660
but does the platform see
it as a positive experience?

00:41:18.660 --> 00:41:20.310
Are you going to keep expanding

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:23.730
so millions of users will give input

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:24.990
about issues of today?

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:26.532
What do you think the future of this is?

00:41:26.532 --> 00:41:27.630
- Yeah, I think it's,

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:28.780
I mean, great question.

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:34.500
So it is interesting because,
actually, in one of our pilots

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:37.080
to establish proof of concept of scale,

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:38.550
the Metaverse Community Forum,

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:41.010
which I mentioned, which a pilot,

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:42.000
it was a year ago,

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:44.520
actually, it was larger
than this more recent one

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:45.390
on generative AI.

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:48.060
It was 6,000 people across 30 countries

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:49.530
in 23 languages.

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:52.260
What we understood may have been,

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:54.270
although I can't necessarily
prove the negative,

00:41:54.270 --> 00:41:57.120
but may have been the
largest deliberative exercise

00:41:57.120 --> 00:41:59.640
at once in human history.

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:02.400
And so certainly scale the technology

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:04.650
allows for scale that is,

00:42:04.650 --> 00:42:09.450
I think, probably rises to the
needs of this moment, right,

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:13.590
where trust in institutions are collapsing

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:14.760
kind of globally, right?

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:16.710
And so, and there's less and less

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:17.880
confidence in institutions.

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:19.620
And so that may require,

00:42:19.620 --> 00:42:22.773
and particularly in emerging technologies,

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:25.500
that no no boundaries may require

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:27.480
global solutions or global input.

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:29.310
So I think the technology, you know,

00:42:29.310 --> 00:42:32.070
we think that it should
be a part of the solution

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:36.120
because these technologies
are transformative

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:37.830
in what they can bring to society,

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:40.080
but they're also, as we've
seen in recent history,

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:42.570
transformative in some of the risks.

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:46.020
And so we have been
really kind of thrilled

00:42:46.020 --> 00:42:48.450
with the way that this enables scale.

00:42:48.450 --> 00:42:50.970
Again, it can also create unevenness.

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:53.790
But there's also other
experiments happening as well.

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:58.710
So I have to acknowledge kind
of other industry efforts

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:00.240
to democratize inputs.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:02.580
Anthropic recently published a blog

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:05.130
where they used the software Polis

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:08.640
to do something called
Collective Constitutional AI.

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:12.063
So Anthropic kind of
released their constitution,

00:43:13.233 --> 00:43:14.100
maybe a year ago,

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:15.570
called Constitutional AI

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:19.020
that underpinned kind of
the rules and the paradigm

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:23.670
in which their chatbot Claude
will operate based off of.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:25.260
And more recently, they used Polis,

00:43:25.260 --> 00:43:27.990
which is a kind of real time

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:31.740
AI-facilitated crowdsourcing tool,

00:43:31.740 --> 00:43:34.800
to gather more perspectives from people

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:38.370
and then inform kind of a
different Constitutional AI.

00:43:38.370 --> 00:43:43.370
So, the Minister of Digital
Affairs in Taiwan, Audrey Tang,

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:46.260
is doing something similar using,

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:48.480
not just related to AI,

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:52.983
but using Polis software to
crowdsource her populace,

00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:55.950
the Taiwanese population,

00:43:55.950 --> 00:43:58.590
to inform kind of where there's alignment

00:43:58.590 --> 00:43:59.790
on certain policy issues

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:01.170
or where there's polarization.

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:03.660
And so, you know,

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:05.700
yes, I think Meta were
really, really excited

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:06.600
about the community forum.

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:08.250
We're really excited about this platform

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:10.800
that can enable discourse at scale.

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:12.540
We obviously saw during the pandemic,

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:13.920
because Zoom,

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:17.460
there was Zoom fatigue, famously,

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:19.710
but even if it's not
that particular platform

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:21.870
or that particular model,

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:24.450
there's other organizations
experimenting with technology.

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:26.880
I think it has to be the future.

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:28.230
But in the spirit of innovation

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:29.790
and everything I've heard here,

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:33.000
I think that it would be
anyone that's in this space

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:34.200
to really iterate further.

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:36.630
And I was really struck
by Federica's comments

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:38.580
earlier in her talk about, you know,

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:42.750
maybe the model isn't necessarily
the same in every region,

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:45.090
maybe you need to meet
regions where they are

00:44:45.090 --> 00:44:46.710
based on their cultural context.

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:50.100
And so maybe you have a
much more flexible model,

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:51.570
all enabled by technology maybe,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:53.010
but maybe you change the model

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:54.060
depending on where you are,

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:56.299
depending about those individual needs.

00:44:56.299 --> 00:44:58.327
- [Announcer] Can I have
your attention, please?

00:44:58.327 --> 00:45:00.450
- Actually, I was about to wrap up.

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:01.560
So anyways, I would just say,

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:05.010
I think technology is super exciting,

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:07.140
also, you know, relatively untested,

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:09.360
and we should continue to innovate.

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.310
- Federica, do you have a follow up on

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:14.040
kind of digital collective governance

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:16.620
or anything about the new online sphere

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:18.240
we find ourselves in?

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:21.840
- Yeah, and maybe this will
sound peculiar said by me,

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:24.420
but I think that the idea that we would

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.853
sort of like a self defeatingly,

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:31.020
eliminate the affordances
that technology gives us

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.990
for participation, for collaboration,

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:37.170
would be absurd and detrimental, I think.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:40.590
And so in a world in which we necessarily

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.600
sort of engage with technology

00:45:42.600 --> 00:45:45.720
to achieve these types of radically,

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:48.330
perhaps, decentralized and at scale forms

00:45:48.330 --> 00:45:50.340
of collaboration and participation,

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:53.280
I think that, as Kris
has already suggested,

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:57.150
we need to be very much
aware of the pitfalls.

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:59.550
And I think that there is
one particular pitfalls

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:01.260
that I am concerned about

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:03.240
when it comes to technology-mediated,

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:05.730
particularly generative AI-mediated

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:08.193
collaboration and participation, which is,

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:11.910
essentially, like what both Philip

00:46:11.910 --> 00:46:14.340
and Forest earlier discussed,

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:17.790
at the heart of the sort of engagement

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:18.780
and participation that they see,

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.640
which is this texture of civic norms

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:23.550
and of just like collaborative norms

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:26.550
that is built, I think, more likely

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:28.770
in spaces where people come together,

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:30.000
physically, and engage.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.400
And now, this might be
empirically incorrect.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:37.400
I stand to be sort of like a proven false,

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:41.220
but I think that it is
something truly to consider

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:44.400
the degree to which
online virtual engagements

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:47.220
make us even more exposed
to the possibility

00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:49.740
of missing out on this fundamental

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:51.780
sort of like a texture of social norms.

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:54.180
That is essentially
what brings us together

00:46:54.180 --> 00:46:55.860
and what makes democracy work.

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:57.720
And so that's, I think,

00:46:57.720 --> 00:47:02.460
something that any use of
technology in these spaces

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:04.143
would have to consider.
- Hmm.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:07.530
- And the last question,
anyone can take this

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:09.150
before we open it up to the audience.

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:12.210
So technological advances
means it's easier

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.760
to talk to folks who are
not physically present.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.400
So maybe our communication
ability has increased.

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:19.770
But we are also in a world,

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:21.810
many places, there's
a lot of polarization,

00:47:21.810 --> 00:47:24.360
and particularly, effective
polarization, right?

00:47:24.360 --> 00:47:25.890
So we seem like we're more divided

00:47:25.890 --> 00:47:27.240
even though it's easier to talk.

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:30.240
And when we think about who participates

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:32.100
in deliberative mini-publics,

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:34.710
who responds to the
invitation to go debate

00:47:34.710 --> 00:47:36.180
with fellow citizens for a weekend,

00:47:36.180 --> 00:47:37.950
giving up kind time and effort,

00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:39.630
should we be worried that...

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:42.090
Maybe not in all places,
but in some countries,

00:47:42.090 --> 00:47:43.200
the US in particular.

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:44.910
It's, you know, it's
more common in Europe,

00:47:44.910 --> 00:47:47.970
all of these institutions,
than it is in the US.

00:47:47.970 --> 00:47:50.070
Is this something we
should be concerned about?

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:52.140
Do people not wanna talk
to each other anymore?

00:47:52.140 --> 00:47:53.820
And so either the practitioners

00:47:53.820 --> 00:47:55.140
who have gotten people together

00:47:55.140 --> 00:47:56.430
or academics who have studied

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:58.860
and thought about
polarization at institutions,

00:47:58.860 --> 00:47:59.910
anyone can take this,

00:47:59.910 --> 00:48:01.830
but should we be worried that polarization

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:05.013
will slow down the benefits
of deliberative democracy?

00:48:09.151 --> 00:48:10.651
- I'll take that.
- Go ahead.

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:15.480
- I think one of the
dangers of polarization

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:20.480
is this kind of siloed
learning through reinforcement

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:22.710
by passively observing others

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:24.540
rather than engaging
deliberatively with them,

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:27.990
and passively observing others
who are essentially like us.

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:29.610
I think it's the kind of thing that,

00:48:29.610 --> 00:48:31.050
you know, that leads...

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:33.690
It's less about immediate affect

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:38.200
and more about arriving at
posterior beliefs and positions

00:48:39.062 --> 00:48:40.380
that are potentially mistaken,

00:48:40.380 --> 00:48:42.690
that are influenced by signals from others

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:44.250
that are not independent signals,

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:46.080
that are sort of from the set of people

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:48.030
who are very much like us.

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:52.890
It's the kind of thing that
leads to people arriving at,

00:48:52.890 --> 00:48:54.630
people coming to view themselves

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:56.760
as having very strong posteriors

00:48:56.760 --> 00:49:00.990
such that there's no point
in engaging in deliberation.

00:49:00.990 --> 00:49:02.190
So it's a substitution

00:49:02.190 --> 00:49:04.080
of kind of passively reinforced beliefs

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:06.963
for proactively searching
debate or conversation.

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:10.590
It's a process that generates

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:13.260
relatively stable contentious posteriors

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:16.560
without turning into kind of a contentious

00:49:16.560 --> 00:49:19.110
but open-ended and possibly
corrective discourse.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:21.780
I'm worried about that as
an effect of polarization.

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:23.910
So it's not so much an effect,

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:27.480
but this kind of effect on how we perceive

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:28.920
the nature of the disagreement

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:31.503
and our own beliefs relative
to the disagreement.

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:38.310
- Phil, have you seen this
in your practitioning?

00:49:38.310 --> 00:49:40.500
- I would say, being at Bard,

00:49:40.500 --> 00:49:43.863
a progressive liberal arts
college in the Hudson Valley,

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:48.660
I've been of two minds.

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:51.060
One is we need to put out the call

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:54.780
as quickly and as strongly as possible

00:49:54.780 --> 00:49:59.670
that there are other modes
of engaging in democracy.

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:00.720
Citizens' assemblies are,

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:03.510
it's a sort of call to action, right?

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:05.850
The issue is that the people
who are primed for that,

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:09.510
even at this point, primed for
even the word for democracy,

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:14.510
are somewhere to the left of
where the right is right now.

00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:15.920
So...

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:19.080
You're...

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:22.380
It's an uphill battle to sort
of reach across the aisle,

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:25.290
and sometimes it feels
like a naive attempt

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:26.370
or like, let's say this,

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:30.330
I try to avoid the sort of
the partisan polarization,

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:31.710
because I think if you get
in like, "Oh, we're gonna try

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:34.110
to get the reds and the
blues in the room together,"

00:50:34.110 --> 00:50:35.160
it kind of misses the point

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:37.470
where it's like most people
probably don't associate

00:50:37.470 --> 00:50:40.170
with, like, as a red or a blue,

00:50:40.170 --> 00:50:41.283
Republican, Democrat.

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:45.120
So if you try to take the
place-based perspective

00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:47.070
and say like, "Anyone out there

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:50.460
who wants to try to create a better,

00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:52.380
sort of more collaborative
form of democracy,"

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:53.940
like even thinking through the language

00:50:53.940 --> 00:50:55.590
on how we talk about citizens' assemblies

00:50:55.590 --> 00:50:57.750
is like, one of the things...

00:50:57.750 --> 00:51:00.538
You know, at first it was the sort,

00:51:00.538 --> 00:51:02.430
it was the sortition project at Bard,

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:03.990
and it was just like no
one knows what that is.

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:05.160
Sounds super academic.

00:51:05.160 --> 00:51:08.670
People are gonna write it off
as like some idea, like, okay.

00:51:08.670 --> 00:51:10.080
Now it's the Democracy Innovation Hub.

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:11.587
After our last conference, we were like,

00:51:11.587 --> 00:51:14.550
"Should we call it the
Center for Citizen Power?"

00:51:14.550 --> 00:51:15.383
You know, like...

00:51:15.383 --> 00:51:17.670
Because there's a question
of how to relate to

00:51:17.670 --> 00:51:20.850
in this rapidly polarized, like, world

00:51:20.850 --> 00:51:23.043
in which language is changed so quickly.

00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:25.590
I think we just have to make the effort

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:27.740
and not worry too much about...

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:30.540
Like, I think if the intention is there

00:51:30.540 --> 00:51:32.070
to meet people where they're at

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:34.890
and to be open about disagreement,

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:36.600
about tough issues,

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:37.950
I think that's the most important thing.

00:51:37.950 --> 00:51:39.450
But I would say, like,

00:51:39.450 --> 00:51:42.450
the people that tend to
come to us or email me

00:51:42.450 --> 00:51:44.940
have like participated in
participatory budgeting, right?

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:46.680
Like, they're...

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:51.030
So there's gotta be an
intention from these efforts

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:52.080
as we're organizing them,

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:54.990
both as citizens or as
educational institutions,

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:57.330
to say, "We're gonna do as best as we can

00:51:57.330 --> 00:52:00.570
to, you know, reach
across different aisles

00:52:00.570 --> 00:52:03.170
or reach across different
sections of the population

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:08.970
without, you know, reproducing
the sort of stereotype

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:12.300
of that idea that already exists."

00:52:12.300 --> 00:52:14.460
I don't know if that helps
answer the question at all.

00:52:14.460 --> 00:52:16.440
- No, and I mean, recruitment
and getting the word out

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:18.450
that there are these positive experiences

00:52:18.450 --> 00:52:20.010
from deliberative mini-publics

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:22.500
or participatory budgeting,

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:23.640
that is also,

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:26.640
the more the work of the
people in this room expands,

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:27.630
the more people will know.

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:30.750
And that's also one of the
goals of this conference.

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:33.510
So we're actually gonna
open it up to the audience.

00:52:33.510 --> 00:52:34.710
So audience members,

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:37.740
if you could raise your hand very high

00:52:37.740 --> 00:52:40.593
and Pam will come and
give you a microphone.

00:52:41.760 --> 00:52:43.710
And please introduce yourself when you...

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:44.670
- So hi, everyone.

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:45.870
I'm Helene Landemore.

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:48.540
I'm professor of Political
Science at Yale University.

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:50.670
I think some of you, at least maybe,

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:53.880
will know me as one of the
optimists about mini-publics

00:52:53.880 --> 00:52:55.890
and pessimists about electoral democracy.

00:52:55.890 --> 00:52:58.470
And thank you so much for this
conference and this panel.

00:52:58.470 --> 00:53:00.690
It's truly extraordinary, I think,

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:02.820
and very symbolically also, I think,

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:04.800
that it's one of the first
conferences on lotteries

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:07.950
that I think has taken place here.

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:09.840
I don't think I organize
one, I should have,

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:12.960
but anyway, that's you.
(everyone laughing)

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:15.663
So I have question for each
of you, if I'm allowed.

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:17.670
So Federica first.

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:21.540
I was wondering if you had
thoughts on how the Greeks,

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:22.373
the ancient Greeks,

00:53:22.373 --> 00:53:24.750
solves the objections raised by Dmitri.

00:53:24.750 --> 00:53:26.460
So how come they,

00:53:26.460 --> 00:53:28.650
for, let's say, 200 years,

00:53:28.650 --> 00:53:29.880
did without elections

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:31.740
and yet were relatively stable

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:35.163
and didn't get captured,
at least, you know,

00:53:37.676 --> 00:53:39.630
in a way that damages our system too much

00:53:39.630 --> 00:53:41.580
by, I don't know, local economic elites

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:43.110
like hot, what.

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:45.510
So Dmitri, it's a question to you as well.

00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:47.820
Does the Greek case answer
some of your questions or not,

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:49.140
or is it a completely different case?

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:50.370
Because we're not talking about

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.830
the transition from an electoral system

00:53:52.830 --> 00:53:55.020
to a, you know, mini-public based system,

00:53:55.020 --> 00:53:56.520
but it's a completely different system

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:59.550
that never had elections
for our political functions.

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:01.533
I have a question for Kris.

00:54:02.402 --> 00:54:03.540
Very nice to meet you.

00:54:03.540 --> 00:54:06.543
I am excited to hear more about
what you're doing at Meta.

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:11.730
So given the objections
from Dmitri, again,

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:12.870
about, you know, what happens

00:54:12.870 --> 00:54:15.357
when you don't give people
real power, in particular,

00:54:15.357 --> 00:54:16.350
and the risk of, you know,

00:54:16.350 --> 00:54:18.300
losing their interest or motivation,

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:21.330
and your own claim that you're
looking to make the opinions

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:23.373
of the participants a bit more binding,

00:54:24.870 --> 00:54:26.100
shouldn't you move towards

00:54:26.100 --> 00:54:28.170
more of a citizens'
assembly model, actually,

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:30.780
where it's not just about
polling people at the end,

00:54:30.780 --> 00:54:33.030
but getting them to
deliberate towards a goal,

00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:34.530
towards a recommendation,

00:54:34.530 --> 00:54:36.000
towards a binding resolution,

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:37.530
and then they would function more

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:39.750
like your Oversight Board, potentially?

00:54:39.750 --> 00:54:40.683
Just a thought.

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:43.620
And also, I have to ask,

00:54:43.620 --> 00:54:45.480
like, what have you learned at Meta

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:49.110
from the OpenAI governance, you know,

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:51.690
or board failure of the last week?

00:54:51.690 --> 00:54:53.580
I would love to hear what you have to say

00:54:53.580 --> 00:54:55.920
because they were
structured as a non-profit

00:54:55.920 --> 00:54:57.930
and that board totally failed.

00:54:57.930 --> 00:55:00.150
You are structured as a for-profit

00:55:00.150 --> 00:55:03.600
and you are seemingly
successfully equipping yourselves

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:07.140
with constraints of some sort.

00:55:07.140 --> 00:55:09.057
I don't know how, you know,
constrained they really are,

00:55:09.057 --> 00:55:10.893
but they look like they could be.

00:55:12.510 --> 00:55:13.833
Dmitri, now,

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:18.090
I would love to be less of a pessimist

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:19.290
about electoral systems

00:55:19.290 --> 00:55:21.660
and so I'm willing to believe

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:23.820
that under some different circumstances,

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:25.830
somewhere, they could be great.

00:55:25.830 --> 00:55:27.840
But I'd like to hear what do you think

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:29.790
is the standard of success,

00:55:29.790 --> 00:55:32.670
and how do you address the
criticism one could make

00:55:32.670 --> 00:55:36.240
based on the sort of a
plutocratic tendencies

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:37.440
of those systems?

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:38.790
There's an article

00:55:38.790 --> 00:55:40.200
in the British Journal
of Political Science

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:41.430
that came out in June

00:55:41.430 --> 00:55:44.550
that shows that across,
like, 30 countries,

00:55:44.550 --> 00:55:47.100
40 years, 2,000 issues,

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:49.740
there's a systematic over representation

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:51.393
or over congruence,

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:56.580
greater congruence with the
preferences of rich people

00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:57.783
in these countries.

00:55:58.830 --> 00:56:01.290
You know, policies match their preferences

00:56:01.290 --> 00:56:02.700
more than they match the preferences

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:04.140
of poor people, for example.

00:56:04.140 --> 00:56:05.520
Or majorities even.

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:08.130
So that seems to be a systematic problem,

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:10.920
and maybe there's a trade off
there that I'm not seeing,

00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:12.990
that we're gaining more competence,

00:56:12.990 --> 00:56:14.310
we're gaining something,

00:56:14.310 --> 00:56:18.000
stability or better
protection of minority,

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.010
you know, in exchange for
that plutocratic bias,

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:23.400
but I would love to hear what it is.

00:56:23.400 --> 00:56:24.510
Did I, oh yeah.

00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:27.300
And Philip, I have a question for you too.

00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:31.380
I hear about your work and how seemingly,

00:56:31.380 --> 00:56:33.150
you know, officials at the city level,

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:34.170
maybe the state level,

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:35.730
are getting somewhat interested

00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:38.040
in mini-publics and lotteries,

00:56:38.040 --> 00:56:39.720
but somehow, at the federal level,

00:56:39.720 --> 00:56:41.347
I find it very dispiriting how,

00:56:41.347 --> 00:56:44.520
you know, the democracy
summits organized by Biden

00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:47.280
systematically failed to
mention deliberative democracy,

00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:48.360
mini-publics, lotteries.

00:56:48.360 --> 00:56:50.010
It's not at all on their agenda.

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:51.390
Why is that?

00:56:51.390 --> 00:56:52.380
And what can be done?

00:56:52.380 --> 00:56:55.050
Thank you. Sorry it was very long, but...

00:56:55.050 --> 00:56:56.490
- Excellent questions.

00:56:56.490 --> 00:56:57.390
We'll just go...

00:56:57.390 --> 00:56:59.310
Federica, if you wanna start,
we'll go down the table.

00:56:59.310 --> 00:57:00.143
- Okay.

00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:03.090
Hi, Helene. Thank you for the question.

00:57:03.090 --> 00:57:05.730
So the Greeks, the Athenians
didn't have elections.

00:57:05.730 --> 00:57:09.690
They used elections to
select a few magistrates.

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:12.663
We don't have evidence of
elections in the large bodies

00:57:12.663 --> 00:57:14.700
that we associate with the democracies,

00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:17.370
the courts, the assembly, and the council.

00:57:17.370 --> 00:57:18.960
But this is, I guess,

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:20.130
it is certainly a situation

00:57:20.130 --> 00:57:21.960
in which there isn't a full fledged

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:23.280
electoral democratic system

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:24.510
that then needs to be replaced

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:27.120
with a lottocratic or other
types of institutions,

00:57:27.120 --> 00:57:29.430
but the Greeks were certainly not unaware

00:57:29.430 --> 00:57:31.410
of elections as a mechanism.

00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:33.850
I think that as far as we can tell

00:57:34.770 --> 00:57:36.480
by looking at people that have studied

00:57:36.480 --> 00:57:38.550
these questions extensively,

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:42.030
democracy without elections
in the vast majority

00:57:42.030 --> 00:57:44.580
of sort of like what is
the decision-making process

00:57:45.633 --> 00:57:48.300
in a situation that didn't lead to,

00:57:48.300 --> 00:57:49.740
either lead capture,

00:57:49.740 --> 00:57:51.420
but also, and I think
that this is the problem

00:57:51.420 --> 00:57:54.480
that the situation in
Athens really generated,

00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:57.990
which is the avoidance of elite capture

00:57:57.990 --> 00:57:59.280
is one aspect of the story,

00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:00.540
the other aspect of the story

00:58:00.540 --> 00:58:03.390
is the avoidance of popular ignorance.

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:06.900
So how do a bunch of
people sitting on a hill

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:09.690
decide on matters of war
and peace and finance

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:10.950
without really being experts?

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:12.390
This is Plato's problem.

00:58:12.390 --> 00:58:14.610
And I think that some of the authors

00:58:14.610 --> 00:58:15.840
that have looked at this question,

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:17.730
including, of course, Josh Ober,

00:58:17.730 --> 00:58:20.190
have really looked at
the political discourse,

00:58:20.190 --> 00:58:22.080
at the texture of civic norms,

00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:25.350
and how very simple
mechanisms for selection,

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:26.790
for decision-making,

00:58:26.790 --> 00:58:29.160
created a situation in which people

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:32.490
shared information and learned

00:58:32.490 --> 00:58:34.500
from coming together in this public body.

00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:37.260
So again, I think that my work today

00:58:37.260 --> 00:58:38.790
is sort of like a pushing against the idea

00:58:38.790 --> 00:58:41.310
that we should continue to
obsess with Ancient Athens,

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:43.620
but others remains a fundamental

00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:45.270
sort of like a place to learn

00:58:45.270 --> 00:58:48.243
about ancient democracies
and pre-modern democracies.

00:58:49.360 --> 00:58:50.463
- Okay, Dmitri.

00:58:52.650 --> 00:58:55.160
- So I'm going to defer to Federica

00:58:55.160 --> 00:58:57.660
on all questions having
to do with Ancient Athens.

00:58:58.620 --> 00:59:01.650
I do want to respond to Helene's question

00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:03.540
about how to think about
the relative advantages,

00:59:03.540 --> 00:59:05.130
at least in principle, relative advantages

00:59:05.130 --> 00:59:06.600
of representative democracy.

00:59:06.600 --> 00:59:09.930
I think one dimension

00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:12.150
where there is something to be said,

00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:14.250
in particular, about representation

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:17.250
has to do with complexity of governance.

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:21.690
I think direct democratic forms,

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:23.370
lottocratic forms are
particularly attractive

00:59:23.370 --> 00:59:27.540
when issue dimensions can be isolated

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:30.120
and dealt with in an isolated way

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:33.690
without generating massive externalities

00:59:33.690 --> 00:59:35.310
with respect to other dimensions.

00:59:35.310 --> 00:59:37.650
I think when you talk about policy making

00:59:37.650 --> 00:59:39.000
that has these kinds of-

00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:40.770
- [Announcer] May I have
your attention, please.

00:59:40.770 --> 00:59:42.810
- So I think when you
talk about policy making

00:59:42.810 --> 00:59:43.950
with respect to issues

00:59:43.950 --> 00:59:46.530
that have these kinds of
cross issue externalities,

00:59:46.530 --> 00:59:49.680
I think that's where there is some value.

00:59:49.680 --> 00:59:50.640
There's considerable value,

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:51.630
I would say, right,

00:59:51.630 --> 00:59:56.070
for something like
representative electoral process

00:59:56.070 --> 01:00:01.070
with policy makers who are
given a jurisdiction right

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:05.410
that encompasses multiple issues.

01:00:05.410 --> 01:00:08.010
And I think that's where, you know...

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:09.150
And I think our world

01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:11.010
is sort of increasingly like that, right?

01:00:11.010 --> 01:00:13.290
Or has been increasingly like that for,

01:00:13.290 --> 01:00:16.050
you know, for, at least for
the last two plus millennia

01:00:16.050 --> 01:00:19.440
since, you know, since Ancient Athens.

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:22.833
So that's the way I would
direct the argument.

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:25.773
- Okay, Phil.
- Okay.

01:00:26.880 --> 01:00:28.330
So I think

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:34.950
we need to run workshops in DC

01:00:34.950 --> 01:00:37.590
with federal government officials,

01:00:37.590 --> 01:00:38.423
and we just...

01:00:38.423 --> 01:00:40.380
Basically, we need to find more folks

01:00:40.380 --> 01:00:43.590
who are willing to organize,

01:00:43.590 --> 01:00:45.420
knock doors, talk,

01:00:45.420 --> 01:00:47.490
call elected officials.

01:00:47.490 --> 01:00:48.780
I think it's already happening,

01:00:48.780 --> 01:00:50.100
and I don't even think,

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:54.030
I know that there's other people out there

01:00:54.030 --> 01:00:56.100
starting to do this work
that haven't connected

01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.260
'cause we haven't had
enough of these convenings.

01:00:58.260 --> 01:01:02.200
So I just came back from DC yesterday

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:06.480
at the National Conference on Citizenship,

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:07.380
the Future of Citizenship,

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.570
and there was a workshop
with Marjan Ehsassi

01:01:09.570 --> 01:01:12.640
and Jillian Youngblood and John Gastil

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:14.820
on citizens' assemblies

01:01:14.820 --> 01:01:16.530
at the National Conference of Citizenship.

01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:20.100
There's now a Democracy Innovation Center

01:01:20.100 --> 01:01:22.590
within the National Civic League.

01:01:22.590 --> 01:01:26.340
And I think, basically,
(announcer faintly speaking)

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:28.110
we need to organize...

01:01:28.110 --> 01:01:29.160
I just...

01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:31.290
I'm like my...
(announcer faintly speaking)

01:01:31.290 --> 01:01:34.110
Yes, I think we basically
need to organize convenings,

01:01:34.110 --> 01:01:35.070
and I think it would be great

01:01:35.070 --> 01:01:36.360
if Bard, and Yale,

01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:38.250
and other colleges thought of this

01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:40.170
from a collaborative
mindset instead, like...

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:41.580
That's the other thing, like,

01:01:41.580 --> 01:01:43.740
all of these institutions are set up

01:01:43.740 --> 01:01:46.290
to have a competitive mindset.

01:01:46.290 --> 01:01:48.007
If we got together and said,

01:01:48.007 --> 01:01:53.007
"How do we scale more
collaborative attempts at this,

01:01:53.820 --> 01:01:55.200
together rather than separately,

01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:57.060
I think we could have a bigger impact."

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:58.500
Obviously, it cannot just be,

01:01:58.500 --> 01:01:59.820
like, elite institutions;

01:01:59.820 --> 01:02:03.870
it's gotta be from a
multi-stakeholder perspective.

01:02:03.870 --> 01:02:05.070
But these convenings

01:02:05.070 --> 01:02:06.840
where anchor institutions have the space

01:02:06.840 --> 01:02:08.940
and the convening space, I think,

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:11.880
I mean, one thing over
the next couple months,

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:14.610
and especially this year, 2024,

01:02:14.610 --> 01:02:16.410
if we can channel some convenings

01:02:16.410 --> 01:02:17.700
not to run citizen assemblies,

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:20.670
but to do things that
are workshopping ideas

01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:21.930
and calling out to people,

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:22.950
'cause this year,

01:02:22.950 --> 01:02:24.753
a lot of people are gonna be like,

01:02:25.687 --> 01:02:27.480
"I don't like either of these candidates.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:30.030
I'm depressed about the way this is going.

01:02:30.030 --> 01:02:33.120
I wish there was some way
for me to get involved."

01:02:33.120 --> 01:02:37.440
So, my pitch is let's convene
a lot of those actors,

01:02:37.440 --> 01:02:39.300
and then bring in the elected officials,

01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:41.073
and do this multi-stakeholder,

01:02:42.720 --> 01:02:44.820
these convenings and workshop ideas,

01:02:44.820 --> 01:02:46.470
from multiple angles.

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:47.940
And I think, we'll be successful.

01:02:47.940 --> 01:02:50.730
I think politicians would...

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:53.490
There are going to be politicians
who start to embrace this

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:55.800
as the next form of democracy

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:59.013
from younger generations, especially.

01:03:00.357 --> 01:03:02.070
- And also for the graduate
students in the room,

01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:03.090
this sounds like a great idea

01:03:03.090 --> 01:03:05.310
for a future dissertation
or future research.

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:06.310
Okay, Kris, finally,

01:03:07.230 --> 01:03:08.063
- Wonderful.

01:03:08.063 --> 01:03:09.360
Thank you for the questions.

01:03:09.360 --> 01:03:10.620
Really nice to meet you.

01:03:10.620 --> 01:03:12.990
I am, as I've mentioned,
a neophyte in this space,

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:14.670
but very, have followed your work

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:16.533
and very much an admirer.

01:03:17.910 --> 01:03:20.040
So on the citizens' assembly

01:03:20.040 --> 01:03:21.210
or the construct that we're using,

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:23.730
deliberative polling
versus citizens' assembly,

01:03:23.730 --> 01:03:26.910
I think, open to innovation, right?

01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:29.010
I think there's a couple different factors

01:03:30.859 --> 01:03:33.600
that are unique to a corporate setting

01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:34.950
that we just need to be mindful of

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:36.870
when creating these types of mechanisms.

01:03:36.870 --> 01:03:38.460
As far as I know,

01:03:38.460 --> 01:03:40.127
there's not a lot of
companies that have done this.

01:03:40.127 --> 01:03:42.540
And so I think we're trying
to find our way through it

01:03:42.540 --> 01:03:45.240
in a way that is as
productive as possible,

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:46.540
that gives, that honors

01:03:47.499 --> 01:03:49.590
the spirit of these mechanisms,

01:03:49.590 --> 01:03:52.290
while also recognizing the,
like, unique constraints

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:54.360
of operating in a for-profit company.

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:57.840
And so the deliberative polling method

01:03:57.840 --> 01:04:01.320
seems to be efficient, scalable,

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:04.950
kind of also recognizes that
the nature of technology

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:06.090
is so fast evolving.

01:04:06.090 --> 01:04:08.070
And so the questions
that we might be asking

01:04:08.070 --> 01:04:09.810
may not necessarily be the same things

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:13.080
as a government may be asking citizens

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:15.390
to weigh in on a citizens' assembly.

01:04:15.390 --> 01:04:17.340
The generative AI community
forum, for example,

01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:18.330
is much more directional

01:04:18.330 --> 01:04:20.640
since generative AI is different

01:04:20.640 --> 01:04:21.930
than it was six months ago,

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:24.600
it's gonna be different in
six months in the future,

01:04:24.600 --> 01:04:26.880
and so we are looking
for directional feedback

01:04:26.880 --> 01:04:29.973
rather than some sort of
specific policy outcome.

01:04:30.840 --> 01:04:32.640
But I think, you know,

01:04:32.640 --> 01:04:36.000
we have not put to bed
any other type of model,

01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:37.320
and I think we're very open to it.

01:04:37.320 --> 01:04:40.020
So we'd love to continue the conversation,

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:41.940
but this, we think, is
kind of the first step

01:04:41.940 --> 01:04:44.580
towards democratizing inputs

01:04:44.580 --> 01:04:47.760
in a way that elevates user voice,

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:51.810
but it does so at the
onset of our innovation

01:04:51.810 --> 01:04:53.160
rather than something that's a redress

01:04:53.160 --> 01:04:56.193
or a recourse mechanism.

01:04:58.260 --> 01:04:59.760
On the OpenAI thing,

01:04:59.760 --> 01:05:01.050
you know, corporate structure,

01:05:01.050 --> 01:05:02.220
I'm not really an expert in that.

01:05:02.220 --> 01:05:05.190
I understand the non-profit
versus for-profit structure.

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:07.500
I think, for me, it
just actually reaffirms

01:05:07.500 --> 01:05:11.400
the need to infuse democratic inputs,

01:05:11.400 --> 01:05:13.830
infuse governance into innovation.

01:05:13.830 --> 01:05:15.030
Because if you're relying

01:05:15.030 --> 01:05:17.070
on a corporate structure to do that,

01:05:17.070 --> 01:05:18.750
like a non-profit board,

01:05:18.750 --> 01:05:20.163
that clearly is not,

01:05:22.520 --> 01:05:26.040
it's not impervious to potential corrupt,

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:26.873
I don't wanna say corruption,

01:05:26.873 --> 01:05:28.710
but potential challenges, right?

01:05:28.710 --> 01:05:31.140
And so I think, if anything,

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:32.760
it just shows that we need to actually

01:05:32.760 --> 01:05:34.890
imbue our innovation with governance,

01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:36.940
and that's why I'm excited to work on it.

01:05:38.490 --> 01:05:41.040
- Okay. Other questions from the audience?

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:41.873
Kevin.

01:05:44.700 --> 01:05:45.533
- Thanks. Yes.

01:05:45.533 --> 01:05:47.340
This is fascinating and interesting

01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:49.390
to have all these different perspectives.

01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:51.960
So my question might,

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:54.120
it's coming from a little
bit from this question

01:05:54.120 --> 01:05:55.950
about sort of the top down,

01:05:55.950 --> 01:05:58.650
why is there no interest in
this at the federal level?

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:01.997
So one of the...

01:06:01.997 --> 01:06:04.247
So the sort of question
I would pose is like,

01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.230
absent to revolution,

01:06:07.230 --> 01:06:09.300
how would you get from
here to there, right?

01:06:09.300 --> 01:06:11.310
How would you get from here to lottocracy?

01:06:11.310 --> 01:06:13.680
So think about this in terms of,

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:14.760
absent to revolution,

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:16.410
this is gonna require elected officials

01:06:16.410 --> 01:06:18.180
to transfer authority, right?

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:19.530
They're gonna have to adopt these

01:06:19.530 --> 01:06:21.240
institutions for some reason.

01:06:21.240 --> 01:06:23.550
So that seems, to me, suggest like

01:06:23.550 --> 01:06:26.850
one set of questions that we
might have answers to them

01:06:26.850 --> 01:06:28.470
or wanna have answers to.

01:06:28.470 --> 01:06:31.650
What can deliberate mini-publics
do for elected leaders?

01:06:31.650 --> 01:06:33.570
And I don't just mean things like,

01:06:33.570 --> 01:06:34.920
we can prove our democracy,

01:06:34.920 --> 01:06:37.380
they're not really gonna
care about that, right?

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:39.720
What can it do for them
in a more mercenary way?

01:06:39.720 --> 01:06:43.260
Now, that mercenariness can be
a bunch of different things.

01:06:43.260 --> 01:06:44.370
One thing would be like,

01:06:44.370 --> 01:06:46.620
it takes an issue like
abortion off our plates, right?

01:06:46.620 --> 01:06:48.330
In Ireland, one of the
reason they want to do this

01:06:48.330 --> 01:06:50.010
'cause they did not want
deal with this question,

01:06:50.010 --> 01:06:50.843
and marriage equality,

01:06:50.843 --> 01:06:53.280
that's gonna blow up our party

01:06:53.280 --> 01:06:54.660
if we try to deal with that.

01:06:54.660 --> 01:06:57.750
Another sort of possibility,

01:06:57.750 --> 01:06:59.610
and here is one that

01:06:59.610 --> 01:07:01.020
I know the beginning of the story

01:07:01.020 --> 01:07:02.160
but not the end.

01:07:02.160 --> 01:07:03.780
So Mike Neblo,

01:07:03.780 --> 01:07:05.820
for a while, was doing these.

01:07:05.820 --> 01:07:08.280
It was my understanding he
had members of Congress,

01:07:08.280 --> 01:07:09.660
especially freshmen,

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:12.840
were going to engage in these forums

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.650
where they spoke with their

01:07:15.570 --> 01:07:18.450
randomly selected constituents, right?

01:07:18.450 --> 01:07:19.350
And so...

01:07:19.350 --> 01:07:21.180
'Cause one of the big
challenges here is...

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:22.410
One way of thinking about this is

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:24.570
getting citizens to trust
deliberate mini-publics,

01:07:24.570 --> 01:07:26.910
but what about getting elected officials,

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:28.590
who are the ones who were gonna
have to transfer authority

01:07:28.590 --> 01:07:29.423
to these institutions,

01:07:29.423 --> 01:07:31.740
how do you get them to trust them?

01:07:31.740 --> 01:07:34.710
So I don't know how Mike's
thing has turned out.

01:07:34.710 --> 01:07:37.560
I know he did some pilots,

01:07:37.560 --> 01:07:38.910
but then he thought he
was doing a bigger one.

01:07:38.910 --> 01:07:41.040
So I wonder what you
guys think about that.

01:07:41.040 --> 01:07:43.470
I wonder if you know
how this has turned out,

01:07:43.470 --> 01:07:45.210
maybe other people in the room do.

01:07:45.210 --> 01:07:47.223
So, yeah, so that's sort of my question.

01:07:48.120 --> 01:07:49.560
How can we do that?

01:07:49.560 --> 01:07:50.450
And then...

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:53.670
Because I feel like
that might be a solution

01:07:53.670 --> 01:07:55.260
to some of these questions
about scaling up,

01:07:55.260 --> 01:07:57.000
some of these questions about,

01:07:57.000 --> 01:07:58.200
also the attractiveness, right?

01:07:58.200 --> 01:08:00.210
If there's not a way to
get from here to there,

01:08:00.210 --> 01:08:01.770
if there's not a way
to answer that question

01:08:01.770 --> 01:08:03.660
of what can this do for politicians,

01:08:03.660 --> 01:08:04.560
absent revolution,

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:07.293
then you might be out of luck.

01:08:11.520 --> 01:08:13.110
- I'll answer. You were
looking at me mostly.

01:08:13.110 --> 01:08:15.900
So I was like..
(everyone laughing)

01:08:15.900 --> 01:08:17.400
I think we asked them.

01:08:17.400 --> 01:08:19.050
So what we've been doing
before, and I've been doing,

01:08:19.050 --> 01:08:20.220
is call up the mayor's office,

01:08:20.220 --> 01:08:21.360
we email this other agency,

01:08:21.360 --> 01:08:22.710
we email the Department of Transportation,

01:08:22.710 --> 01:08:23.820
we email the senators,

01:08:23.820 --> 01:08:25.770
we, you know, like, write Joe Biden.

01:08:25.770 --> 01:08:26.910
You know, not write to Joe Biden.

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:27.810
That's a bad idea.

01:08:27.810 --> 01:08:30.750
But basically, what are you,

01:08:30.750 --> 01:08:32.850
what is really hard?

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:34.770
And this is a model.

01:08:34.770 --> 01:08:37.290
We're not willing to just
like spend all of our time

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:39.540
doing something for you as a PR

01:08:39.540 --> 01:08:42.480
or like engagement trick

01:08:42.480 --> 01:08:44.190
that you're trying to run, right?

01:08:44.190 --> 01:08:45.450
Like we're citizens,

01:08:45.450 --> 01:08:48.960
we think this model could
be the future of democracy,

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:51.060
what are you willing
to do power sharing on,

01:08:51.060 --> 01:08:52.110
or to think through?

01:08:52.110 --> 01:08:55.110
Or what is some issue
that could be really hard?

01:08:55.110 --> 01:08:56.250
That is an open question

01:08:56.250 --> 01:08:57.990
that we have to go to the agencies,

01:08:57.990 --> 01:08:59.400
and we have to...

01:08:59.400 --> 01:09:01.980
Like, basically, the things we've learned

01:09:01.980 --> 01:09:03.780
from the folks in like Australia

01:09:03.780 --> 01:09:06.210
or healthy democracy, all, you know,

01:09:06.210 --> 01:09:07.350
we've been learning from folks

01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:09.360
who've been doing this for decades,

01:09:09.360 --> 01:09:10.717
and they go and they ask,

01:09:10.717 --> 01:09:12.450
"What is really difficult?"

01:09:12.450 --> 01:09:15.003
Like, what's the most
difficult thing you face?

01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:17.460
And then they workshop different topics,

01:09:17.460 --> 01:09:18.720
and then you have to
figure out trade offs,

01:09:18.720 --> 01:09:19.553
and you think...

01:09:20.400 --> 01:09:21.870
I think that starts a conversation,

01:09:21.870 --> 01:09:23.340
and then other conversations open up.

01:09:23.340 --> 01:09:24.173
Like, we've been working

01:09:24.173 --> 01:09:25.770
with the Mayor's Office
of Engagement in New York

01:09:25.770 --> 01:09:27.541
and it turns out that, you know,

01:09:27.541 --> 01:09:28.920
that's a unique office

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:31.890
because they serve as a coordinating role

01:09:31.890 --> 01:09:34.650
for all public engagement
up from the city side.

01:09:34.650 --> 01:09:35.790
So they have unique insights,

01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:36.630
and they say, "Well, you know..."

01:09:36.630 --> 01:09:38.220
So there's a whole
conversation that opens up,

01:09:38.220 --> 01:09:40.440
and then there's a conversation
of how do we, as citizens,

01:09:40.440 --> 01:09:42.300
relate to this office, right?

01:09:42.300 --> 01:09:44.400
'Cause it's trust building.

01:09:44.400 --> 01:09:46.770
When we started doing it, I honestly,

01:09:46.770 --> 01:09:49.140
we've built trust in a way,

01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:51.150
in having that conversation

01:09:51.150 --> 01:09:52.230
from the inside and the outside.

01:09:52.230 --> 01:09:53.070
So I think, like,

01:09:53.070 --> 01:09:55.260
we basically need to reach out

01:09:55.260 --> 01:09:58.350
and ask them how, you know,

01:09:58.350 --> 01:10:00.510
what are the ways they would
interact with this model?

01:10:00.510 --> 01:10:02.310
And then we learn, oh, some people

01:10:02.310 --> 01:10:04.260
want to use it for bad reasons.

01:10:04.260 --> 01:10:05.400
We don't wanna work with them.

01:10:05.400 --> 01:10:07.860
Some people have really good ideas.

01:10:07.860 --> 01:10:09.570
Maybe there's already an existing thing

01:10:09.570 --> 01:10:11.850
that they're doing in their
agency that is a lot like this

01:10:11.850 --> 01:10:13.710
or some citizen oversight board

01:10:13.710 --> 01:10:15.570
that we could help them implement.

01:10:15.570 --> 01:10:16.830
It doesn't have to be
a citizens' assembly.

01:10:16.830 --> 01:10:18.180
It's more like,

01:10:18.180 --> 01:10:19.290
let's meet people where they're at

01:10:19.290 --> 01:10:20.910
and workshop these ideas.

01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:22.740
That's been our approach.

01:10:22.740 --> 01:10:24.630
- And also, sorry, if I can just jump in.

01:10:24.630 --> 01:10:26.550
One model to look at is Belgium

01:10:26.550 --> 01:10:27.870
that has deliberative committees

01:10:27.870 --> 01:10:29.880
that bring together elected politicians

01:10:29.880 --> 01:10:33.000
and randomly selected citizens
to debate with each other.

01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:34.740
So politicians are going to learn about

01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:36.720
the efficacy of deliberative democracy.

01:10:36.720 --> 01:10:37.800
That is one way

01:10:37.800 --> 01:10:40.200
where if you treat all
of those politicians,

01:10:40.200 --> 01:10:41.520
or at least we should
be looking to Belgium

01:10:41.520 --> 01:10:42.840
to see if politicians,

01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:44.700
those politicians who have experienced

01:10:44.700 --> 01:10:47.310
deliberative committees
are more likely to support

01:10:47.310 --> 01:10:50.640
the implementation of this
institution in other levels.

01:10:50.640 --> 01:10:53.040
Okay, another question.

01:10:53.040 --> 01:10:54.750
Oh, I'm sorry. Yeah, Dmitri, go ahead.

01:10:54.750 --> 01:10:56.160
- I think deliberative mini-publics

01:10:56.160 --> 01:10:58.367
give an opportunity to politicians

01:10:58.367 --> 01:11:02.770
who do not want to pander,
right, to an opportunity

01:11:04.610 --> 01:11:08.250
to create an independent
source of information

01:11:08.250 --> 01:11:12.150
that might have some standing
among their constituency

01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:14.430
and enable them to avoid pandering.

01:11:14.430 --> 01:11:16.380
So I think there is that kind of an...

01:11:16.380 --> 01:11:18.150
Obviously, it's not true
of every politician;

01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:20.160
it's true of politicians who, you know,

01:11:20.160 --> 01:11:22.950
who would welcome an
opportunity to take a position

01:11:22.950 --> 01:11:24.600
that's different from the
one that they're taking,

01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:26.880
and that could enable them to do that.

01:11:26.880 --> 01:11:28.170
- I have just something

01:11:28.170 --> 01:11:29.940
slight to add onto that.
- Go ahead.

01:11:29.940 --> 01:11:31.740
Aside from my role at Meta,

01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:32.970
at University of Maryland,

01:11:32.970 --> 01:11:35.190
I believe his name's Steven Kull,

01:11:35.190 --> 01:11:36.660
runs the Voice of the People,

01:11:36.660 --> 01:11:39.150
which is a deliberative poll outfit.

01:11:39.150 --> 01:11:41.490
They specifically engage constituents

01:11:41.490 --> 01:11:46.380
and do deliberative polls
to address polarization,

01:11:46.380 --> 01:11:49.200
to show members of Congress that actually,

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:51.210
when given information

01:11:51.210 --> 01:11:52.980
and opportunity to
engage with one another,

01:11:52.980 --> 01:11:54.420
people are not actually as polarized,

01:11:54.420 --> 01:11:55.743
and to, hopefully,

01:11:57.869 --> 01:11:59.820
to encourage members of Congress

01:11:59.820 --> 01:12:02.160
to change positions on certain issues.

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:05.100
So it's not the same, but just another way

01:12:05.100 --> 01:12:07.770
that people are using deliberation

01:12:07.770 --> 01:12:10.923
to support the overall kind of system.

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:14.120
- Okay, we have time
for one more question.

01:12:14.120 --> 01:12:16.170
Do we have another question?

01:12:16.170 --> 01:12:18.360
Yeah, right there, Pam.

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:19.193
Eliza.

01:12:20.850 --> 01:12:22.170
- Hey, I'm Eliza Oak,

01:12:22.170 --> 01:12:25.680
PhD candidate in Poli Sci here at Yale.

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:28.350
So I'm curious how you think about

01:12:28.350 --> 01:12:30.450
which decisions are better suited

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:33.240
for collective decision-making
in the first place,

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:36.090
as opposed to which
decisions should be made

01:12:36.090 --> 01:12:39.390
by a smaller group of
experts and representatives.

01:12:39.390 --> 01:12:41.100
For example, do you think,

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:43.440
you know, we should optimize around

01:12:43.440 --> 01:12:46.290
what are the most controversial decisions

01:12:46.290 --> 01:12:47.490
that the public's divided on

01:12:47.490 --> 01:12:49.170
in hopes of these discussions

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:51.600
could kind of, you know,
encourage dialogue,

01:12:51.600 --> 01:12:53.070
broader consensus,

01:12:53.070 --> 01:12:54.810
or, of course, there's
other considerations,

01:12:54.810 --> 01:12:57.150
maybe, you know,

01:12:57.150 --> 01:13:00.150
making sure the topic is
sufficiently approachable

01:13:00.150 --> 01:13:01.530
and not too complex.

01:13:01.530 --> 01:13:03.420
But I'm curious, especially
for the practitioners,

01:13:03.420 --> 01:13:05.270
how you think about that in practice.

01:13:07.380 --> 01:13:09.180
- Do you want it?
- No, no, please.

01:13:10.170 --> 01:13:12.930
- Thanks, Eliza, and I'm a
big fan of your work too.

01:13:12.930 --> 01:13:15.570
I love the article that
you wrote with Andy Hall,

01:13:15.570 --> 01:13:17.580
and you can teach me about web3

01:13:17.580 --> 01:13:20.853
and decentralized governance anytime.

01:13:22.650 --> 01:13:23.790
Maybe I can just share a little bit

01:13:23.790 --> 01:13:24.810
of how we're thinking about it.

01:13:24.810 --> 01:13:26.670
I think, probably, it's gonna
be a little bit different

01:13:26.670 --> 01:13:31.670
from some folks that focus
more on public institutions.

01:13:32.100 --> 01:13:34.830
The way that we have
thought about it to date is,

01:13:34.830 --> 01:13:37.140
because, actually, right now,

01:13:37.140 --> 01:13:40.040
I mean, there's obviously
things that are highly contested

01:13:40.950 --> 01:13:43.140
around social media and technology,

01:13:43.140 --> 01:13:46.200
but because a lot of the
technology is emerging,

01:13:46.200 --> 01:13:47.880
what we've found is actually like,

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:49.470
you know, things on the Metaverse,

01:13:49.470 --> 01:13:50.820
or things even on generative AI,

01:13:50.820 --> 01:13:52.950
they're not super polarized yet.

01:13:52.950 --> 01:13:56.940
So for us, we were looking
for a lot of gray space

01:13:56.940 --> 01:13:58.410
where there's not really necessarily

01:13:58.410 --> 01:14:00.270
a clear guiding perspective

01:14:00.270 --> 01:14:02.640
either in public or at the company.

01:14:02.640 --> 01:14:05.610
So there's may not polarized perspectives,

01:14:05.610 --> 01:14:08.490
but maybe just like a lack of consensus

01:14:08.490 --> 01:14:11.520
where we could explore and probe.

01:14:11.520 --> 01:14:14.820
Again, coming from a
for-profit organization,

01:14:14.820 --> 01:14:16.260
also something that we're confident

01:14:16.260 --> 01:14:18.420
that we would actually want to

01:14:18.420 --> 01:14:20.520
hear public input on, right?

01:14:20.520 --> 01:14:22.110
There are some decisions

01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:24.150
that the company is gonna say

01:14:24.150 --> 01:14:27.150
is within the company's domain,

01:14:27.150 --> 01:14:31.410
but that we would actually
be able to valuable,

01:14:31.410 --> 01:14:35.340
actually be able to solicit and put on.

01:14:35.340 --> 01:14:38.130
And then the other one is around,

01:14:38.130 --> 01:14:42.120
you know, how do we
bring participants along,

01:14:42.120 --> 01:14:45.330
and is it something that
it's not so technical

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:47.040
that we could actually, you know,

01:14:47.040 --> 01:14:49.470
enable through deliberation, education,

01:14:49.470 --> 01:14:51.540
and expert engagement

01:14:51.540 --> 01:14:53.460
that people to have a rich discussion

01:14:53.460 --> 01:14:54.870
and give us valuable feedback,

01:14:54.870 --> 01:14:58.140
and something that they can
actually participate in.

01:14:58.140 --> 01:15:00.360
And so, you know, we found,

01:15:00.360 --> 01:15:01.920
certainly, through all of our pilots,

01:15:01.920 --> 01:15:04.170
that we were impressed by the richness

01:15:04.170 --> 01:15:05.940
of the discussions and the deliberations

01:15:05.940 --> 01:15:08.160
even on things that are pretty technical.

01:15:08.160 --> 01:15:10.560
But you can imagine that coming
from a technology company,

01:15:10.560 --> 01:15:11.670
there are things that are so,

01:15:11.670 --> 01:15:13.943
and actually things like web3,

01:15:13.943 --> 01:15:15.870
I mean, I'm not convinced that everyone

01:15:15.870 --> 01:15:17.970
would be able to grasp
every technical detail

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:19.020
about our technology,

01:15:19.020 --> 01:15:22.293
and so finding something
that is fit for purpose,

01:15:23.160 --> 01:15:26.040
for deliberation in terms
of the accessibility

01:15:26.040 --> 01:15:29.430
of the topic as well.
- Okay. Phil, final response.

01:15:29.430 --> 01:15:30.570
A brief one if you can.

01:15:30.570 --> 01:15:32.843
- Yeah, I'd just point to Forest.

01:15:32.843 --> 01:15:35.400
I don't think you saw Forest's
presentation this morning,

01:15:35.400 --> 01:15:36.233
but it's recorded.

01:15:36.233 --> 01:15:38.310
So he went through sort of the way

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:41.190
we thought about a
democratically legitimate way

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:44.970
to arrive at an issue in New York City

01:15:44.970 --> 01:15:47.400
around energy and climate, for instance,

01:15:47.400 --> 01:15:51.420
which you might have some formal body,

01:15:51.420 --> 01:15:52.350
team of experts,

01:15:52.350 --> 01:15:55.770
whether it's the New
York version of the IPCC,

01:15:59.010 --> 01:16:00.720
deliver sort of the,

01:16:00.720 --> 01:16:03.990
or a team of university sort of professors

01:16:03.990 --> 01:16:06.480
who have studied the
biggest climate impacts

01:16:06.480 --> 01:16:07.650
or the biggest challenges

01:16:07.650 --> 01:16:09.900
facing the energy system
in New York, right?

01:16:09.900 --> 01:16:11.853
Or the energy transition.

01:16:13.110 --> 01:16:16.080
And then, you know, list out those five,

01:16:16.080 --> 01:16:18.300
and then have a citywide poll of some sort

01:16:18.300 --> 01:16:19.470
or an engagement mechanism

01:16:19.470 --> 01:16:20.707
in which everyday people are saying,

01:16:20.707 --> 01:16:22.500
"Oh, we wanna be involved with that."

01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:23.930
They've also done this in...

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:26.881
Oh, I'm actually blanking

01:16:26.881 --> 01:16:28.830
on where they did this, but basically...

01:16:28.830 --> 01:16:30.390
Oh, in Belgium, I think.

01:16:30.390 --> 01:16:31.320
On the deliberative committees,

01:16:31.320 --> 01:16:32.170
if I'm not wrong.

01:16:33.090 --> 01:16:33.990
Going back and forth,

01:16:33.990 --> 01:16:35.670
where the politicians
say, "We're willing,"

01:16:35.670 --> 01:16:37.237
again, you go into the politicians,

01:16:37.237 --> 01:16:39.300
"We're willing to work
on these five issues."

01:16:39.300 --> 01:16:40.350
And then taking those five,

01:16:40.350 --> 01:16:43.290
asking the public of these five
"what's the most important"

01:16:43.290 --> 01:16:44.940
then maybe going back
to the experts, right?

01:16:44.940 --> 01:16:46.290
So there's a...

01:16:46.290 --> 01:16:47.310
Oh, I'm supposed to be short,

01:16:47.310 --> 01:16:48.600
so I'll stop there.

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:51.840
But I think designing
a democratic mechanism

01:16:51.840 --> 01:16:53.070
to arrive at that,

01:16:53.070 --> 01:16:55.290
and doing engagement with that

01:16:55.290 --> 01:16:56.880
rather than there just being some topic

01:16:56.880 --> 01:16:57.960
that comes like a UFO,

01:16:57.960 --> 01:16:59.640
like the immediate thing

01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:00.990
if there's not a good messenger is like,

01:17:00.990 --> 01:17:03.333
who chose this topic, right, I think.

01:17:04.866 --> 01:17:06.930
- And so, regretfully, we are out of time,

01:17:06.930 --> 01:17:08.760
but thankfully, this panel

01:17:08.760 --> 01:17:11.100
and the entire conference
today has been recorded.

01:17:11.100 --> 01:17:14.250
And you can find information
about the conference,

01:17:14.250 --> 01:17:16.170
contact details for the presenters,

01:17:16.170 --> 01:17:19.890
and the video clips on
the Yale ISPS website

01:17:19.890 --> 01:17:21.780
under the Democratic Innovations Program.

01:17:21.780 --> 01:17:23.010
I'd like to take this opportunity

01:17:23.010 --> 01:17:25.440
to, again, thank Yale ISPS,

01:17:25.440 --> 01:17:27.660
Alan Gerber, Pam, and Kira

01:17:27.660 --> 01:17:29.340
for helping us organize this conference,

01:17:29.340 --> 01:17:30.990
and thank you so much to our panelists

01:17:30.990 --> 01:17:33.195
and all participants. Yeah.
- Thank you, Ali.

01:17:33.195 --> 01:17:36.195
(everyone clapping)

01:17:41.237 --> 01:17:43.820
(gentle music)

